{:edb/db-before-t 190 :edb/db-after-t 191 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533503 50 #inst "2026-10-11T03:52:49.151Z" 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405048 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050295 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405048 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050866 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405049 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406011 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405049 1071 "Source question: 2.\n\n**Question 2**\n\nFind the tangent line for $x(t)=11t$ and $y(t)=t^2-2$ at $t=6$. Give an exact answer.\n\n$y(x)=$ ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405049 1071 "Source question: 2.\n\n**Question 2**\n\nFind the tangent line for $x(t)=11t$ and $y(t)=t^2-2$ at $t=6$. Give an exact answer.\n\n$y(x)=$ {{field-1}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405049 1074 "The slope is $(2t)/11$, so at $t=6$ it is $12/11$. The point is $(66,34)$, giving $y-34=(12/11)(x-66)$ and $y=12x/11-38$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405053 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406098 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405053 1071 "**Question 11 — Part A**\n\nHow are the field vectors directed?" 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405053 1071 "**Question 11 — Part A**\n\nHow are the field vectors directed?\n\n1. Parallel to the $y$-axis.\n\n2. Parallel to the radius vectors of circles centered at the origin.\n\n3. Parallel to the $x$-axis.\n\n4. Tangent to circles centered at the origin." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405053 1074 "The field vector is perpendicular to the radius: $\\langle x,y\\rangle\\cdot\\langle-y,x\\rangle=0$. Therefore it is tangent to the circle through that point, except that the origin has the zero vector." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405054 1074 "$D=\\{(r,\\theta,z):a\\le r\\le b,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le2\\pi,\\ g(r)\\le z\\le f(r)\\}$.\n\nRotation about the $z$-axis replaces the nonnegative horizontal coordinate $x$ by radial distance $r$. One full turn gives the angular range; the lower and upper surfaces retain the original functions evaluated at $r$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405056 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048832 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405056 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049150 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405056 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049168 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405057 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049889 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405060 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406182 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405060 1071 "Source question: 12.\n\nFind the lateral area above the xy-plane and below $z=6xy$, formed by vertical segments from $C: y=\\sqrt{49-x^2},\\ 0\\le x\\le7$.\n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405060 1071 "Source question: 12.\n\nFind the lateral area above the xy-plane and below $z=6xy$, formed by vertical segments from $C: y=\\sqrt{49-x^2},\\ 0\\le x\\le7$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405060 1074 "Use x=7cosθ, y=7sinθ, 0≤θ≤π/2; scalar arc length is orientation independent. Then $A=\\int_0^{\\pi/2}294\\cos\\theta\\sin\\theta\\,(7)d\\theta=2058/2=1029$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405061 1089 #edb/ref 17592186051091 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405062 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049366 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405062 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050033 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405063 1089 #edb/ref 17592186051091 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405066 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048684 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405066 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050246 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405067 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048575 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405067 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049487 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405067 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050614 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405069 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406295 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405069 1071 "Source question: 3.\n\n**Question 3**\n\nFind the tangent line for $x(t)=4/t^2$ and $y(t)=7/t$ at $t=1$. Give an exact answer.\n\n$y(x)=$ ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405069 1071 "Source question: 3.\n\n**Question 3**\n\nFind the tangent line for $x(t)=4/t^2$ and $y(t)=7/t$ at $t=1$. Give an exact answer.\n\n$y(x)=$ {{field-1}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405069 1074 "Differentiate with respect to $t$: $x'=-8/t^3$ and $y'=-7/t^2$. At $t=1$, the slope is $7/8$ and the point is $(4,7)$, so $y-7=(7/8)(x-4)$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405070 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406115 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405070 1071 "Source question: 8.\n\nUse Stokes’ theorem to find $\\oint_C[3y^2dx+3z^2dy+3x^2dz]$, where C is the triangle with vertices (6,0,0), (0,6,0), (0,0,6), oriented counterclockwise.\n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405070 1071 "Source question: 8.\n\nUse Stokes’ theorem to find $\\oint_C[3y^2dx+3z^2dy+3x^2dz]$, where C is the triangle with vertices (6,0,0), (0,6,0), (0,0,6), oriented counterclockwise. Interpret counterclockwise as viewed from above (positive z).\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405070 1074 "The spanning plane is z=6−x−y, with upward vector area (1,1,1)dxdy. Curl F=(−6z,−6x,−6y); its dot product with (1,1,1) is −6(x+y+z)=−36. The xy-projection has area 6·6/2=18, so the integral is −648. The reverse traversal gives +648." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405071 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048819 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405071 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049487 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405071 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050277 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405071 1089 #edb/ref 17592186051090 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405072 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406279 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405072 1071 "Source question: 2.\n\nFind $\\int_C(22y-x^4)\\,ds$ for $x=t,\\ y=6t,\\ 0\\le t\\le1$.\n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405072 1071 "Source question: 2.\n\nFind $\\int_C(22y-x^4)\\,ds$ for $x=t,\\ y=6t,\\ 0\\le t\\le1$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405072 1074 "The speed is $\\sqrt{1+36}=\\sqrt{37}$. Thus $I=\\sqrt{37}\\int_0^1(132t-t^4)dt=\\sqrt{37}(66-1/5)$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405073 1068 #edb/ref 17592186405875 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405073 1071 "**Question 5 — Part B**\n\nPoints with vertical tangents: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405073 1071 "**Question 5 — Part B**\n\nPoints with vertical tangents: {{field-1}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405073 1074 "Vertical tangents occur when $\\sin t=0$ and $\\cos t\\ne0$: $t=0,\\pi,2\\pi$. These give $(0,3)$ and $(20,3)$; the first and last parameter values describe the same point." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405075 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406108 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405075 1071 "Source question: 3.\n\nFind $\\partial(x,y)/\\partial(u,v)$ for $x=2u/(5v)$, $y=8u-4v$.\n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405075 1071 "Source question: 3.\n\nFind $\\partial(x,y)/\\partial(u,v)$ for $x=2u/(5v)$, $y=8u-4v$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405075 1074 "For v≠0, the derivative matrix is $\\begin{pmatrix}2/(5v)&-2u/(5v^2)\\\\8&-4\\end{pmatrix}$. Its determinant is $-8/(5v)+16u/(5v^2)$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405076 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048923 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405076 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050142 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405077 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048960 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405077 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049859 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405078 1074 "For $x>c$, the base $x-c$ is positive, so all displayed real powers are defined and the exponent laws apply. Differentiating gives $y\\prime=a(x-c)^{a-1}$. Meanwhile, $ay^{(a-1)/a}=a[(x-c)^a]^{(a-1)/a}=a(x-c)^{a-1}$. The two sides agree for every $x\\in(c,\\infty)$, verifying the solution.\n\nUse the chain rule, including the derivative $1$ of $x-c$. The condition $a\ne0$ makes the exponent $(a-1)/a$ meaningful; $x>c$ is essential when $a$ is an arbitrary real number." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405079 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048960 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405079 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049859 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405080 1068 #edb/ref 17592186405840 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405080 1071 "Source question: 1.\n\n**Question 1**\n\nFind the tangent line for $x(t)=4t^2$ and $y(t)=t$ at $t=9$. Give an exact answer.\n\n$y(x)=$ ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405080 1071 "Source question: 1.\n\n**Question 1**\n\nFind the tangent line for $x(t)=4t^2$ and $y(t)=t$ at $t=9$. Give an exact answer.\n\n$y(x)=$ {{field-1}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405080 1074 "Use $dy/dx=y'(t)/x'(t)$. At $t=9$, the point is $(324,9)$ and the slope is $1/72$. Thus $y-9=(x-324)/72$, or $y=x/72+9/2$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405083 1074 "$d\\mathbf r=\\langle3,4,5\\tan t\\rangle\\,dt$, so $dx=3\\,dt$, $dy=4\\,dt$, and $dz=5\\tan t\\,dt$.\n\nThe vector differential is $\\mathbf r\\,\\prime(t)\\,dt$. The same scalar increment $dt$ multiplies every component; it is not a fourth component." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405084 1074 "One valid choice is the slice $y=0$, giving $x^2+z^2=1$ in the $xz$-plane. At $(x,y,z)=(0,0,1)$, draw the tangent $z=1$, with slope $dz/dx=0$. At $(1/\\sqrt2,0,1/\\sqrt2)$, draw $z=-x+\\sqrt2$ within the same plane, with slope $-1$. Both agree with $z_x=-x/z$. Show the slice on the sphere; other nondegenerate slices and correctly checked tangent points are valid.\n\n![Authored reference graph](images/47/47d98e418cee25adf036004c66cffb1de67b2898235229ebd28921b92ea5f2cb.png)\n\nFor a slice $y=y_0$, the circle is $x^2+z^2=1-y_0^2$, so choose $|y_0|<1$. Measure slope in the $xz$-plane while keeping $y$ fixed. At $z=0$, the slice tangent is vertical and $-x/z$ is undefined." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405085 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049386 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405085 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049487 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405086 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048960 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405086 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050149 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405086 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050561 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405087 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406254 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405087 1071 "**Question 4 — Part B**\n\nCompute the dot product, then explain why $\\nabla\\varphi$ is perpendicular to the level curve at $(x,y)$.\n\n$\\nabla\\varphi\\cdot\\langle\\varphi_y,-\\varphi_x\\rangle=$ ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405087 1071 "**Question 4 — Part B**\n\nCompute the dot product, then explain why $\\nabla\\varphi$ is perpendicular to the level curve at $(x,y)$.\n\n$\\nabla\\varphi\\cdot\\langle\\varphi_y,-\\varphi_x\\rangle=$ {{field-1}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405087 1074 "The gradient is $\\langle\\varphi_x,\\varphi_y\\rangle$, so the dot product is $\\varphi_x\\varphi_y-\\varphi_y\\varphi_x=0$. At a regular point, the nonzero vector from Part A spans the tangent line; therefore the nonzero gradient is normal to the curve. Include this geometric conclusion with the calculation." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405088 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050537 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405088 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050866 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405089 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406260 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405089 1071 "Source question: 8.\n\nLet $\\mathbf F=\\langle x,y,z\\rangle$ and let S bound a solid E satisfying the divergence theorem, with outward normal n. Use $V=\\frac13\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,dS$ to find the volume of a rectangular parallelepiped with side lengths a,b,c.\n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405089 1071 "Source question: 8.\n\nLet $\\mathbf F=\\langle x,y,z\\rangle$ and let S bound a solid E satisfying the divergence theorem, with outward normal n. Use $V=\\frac13\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,dS$ to find the volume of a rectangular parallelepiped with side lengths a,b,c.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405089 1074 "Place the box in 0≤x≤a, 0≤y≤b, 0≤z≤c. Flux on the three coordinate-plane faces is 0. On x=a the flux is a·bc=abc; the faces y=b and z=c each contribute abc as well. Total flux is 3abc, so dividing by 3 gives V=abc." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405090 1074 "Trace a complete small closed trajectory around the center near $(-2,0)$, following the field continuously through the upper arc, right turn, lower arc, and left turn. Label the whole loop “integral curve, not a solution graph.” The loop has two heights for many $x$ values and vertical turns, so the entire loop cannot be represented by a differentiable single-valued $y(x)$.\n\nThe worksheet uses “integral curve” geometrically for a traced trajectory. Match the field’s tangent directions rather than drawing an arbitrary circle; its exact shape need not be circular. The distinction from part (a) is the whole closed trajectory versus a graph segment." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405092 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406121 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405092 1071 "Source question: 24.\n\nFind the work of $\\mathbf F=e^x\\mathbf i+10y\\mathbf j$ along the oriented curve C shown below.\n\n![Oriented path C](images/3b/3bc389fa33dfa4ae3a4ddbcb9554ef4bc53ba3ce0ddedead9e597759dfacd8e7.png)\n\nThe arrows give (1,0) → (0,1) → (−1,1) → (−1,2).\n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405092 1071 "Source question: 24.\n\nFind the work of $\\mathbf F=e^x\\mathbf i+10y\\mathbf j$ along the oriented curve C shown below.\n\n![Oriented path C](images/3b/3bc389fa33dfa4ae3a4ddbcb9554ef4bc53ba3ce0ddedead9e597759dfacd8e7.png)\n\nThe arrows give (1,0) → (0,1) → (−1,1) → (−1,2).\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405092 1074 "A potential is $\\phi=e^x+5y^2$. Follow the image arrows from (1,0) to (−1,2). Therefore $W=\\phi(-1,2)-\\phi(1,0)=e^{-1}+20-e$. The source image’s automatic alt text misstates an endpoint and reverses traversal; the visible labels and arrows determine this task." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405093 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048152 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405093 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049536 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405096 1074 "$ds=5|\\sec t|\\,|dt|$. On the interval $0\\le t\\le\\pi/3$, with increasing $t$, this is $ds=5\\sec t\\,dt$.\n\n$|d\\mathbf r|=\\sqrt{3^2+4^2+25\\tan^2t}\\,|dt|=5\\sqrt{1+\\tan^2t}\\,|dt|=5|\\sec t|\\,|dt|$. The absolute values express the nonnegative length element." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405100 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406089 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405100 1071 "**Question 9 — Part A**\n\nFind its exact arc length: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405100 1071 "**Question 9 — Part A**\n\nFind its exact arc length: {{field-1}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405100 1074 "The derivatives are $x'=\\cos t-t\\sin t$ and $y'=\\sin t+t\\cos t$, so the squared speed is $1+t^2$. Thus $s=\\int_0^{5\\pi}\\sqrt{1+t^2}\\,dt=\\frac12[5\\pi\\sqrt{1+25\\pi^2}+\\ln(5\\pi+\\sqrt{1+25\\pi^2})]$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405101 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406097 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405101 1071 "Source question: 12.\n\nUse **Green’s theorem** to find the area bounded by $y=x^2$ and $y=x+42$.\n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405101 1071 "Source question: 12.\n\nUse **Green’s theorem** to find the area bounded by $y=x^2$ and $y=x+42$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405101 1074 "The intersections solve x²−x−42=(x−7)(x+6)=0. Use $A=-\\oint_Cy\\,dx$ with positive orientation: lower parabola from −6 to 7, upper line from 7 to −6. Then $A=\\int_{-6}^7(x+42-x^2)dx=2197/6$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405102 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406140 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405102 1071 "Source question: 11.\n\nFind $\\iint_R16x\\cos(15xy)\\,dA$ over the region bounded by $xy=1$, $xy=5$, $x=1$, $x=16$. Use $u=x$, $v=xy$.\n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405102 1071 "Source question: 11.\n\nFind $\\iint_R16x\\cos(15xy)\\,dA$ over the region bounded by $xy=1$, $xy=5$, $x=1$, $x=16$. Use $u=x$, $v=xy$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405102 1074 "Invert: x=u,y=v/u. Since u>0, $|J|=1/u$. The rectangle is 1≤u≤16, 1≤v≤5. Thus $I=\\int_1^{16}\\int_1^5 16\\cos(15v)dvdu=16(\\sin75-\\sin15)$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405104 1068 #edb/ref 17592186405967 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405104 1071 "Source question: 8.\n\n**Question 8**\n\nFind the arc length for $x(t)=13\\sin t$, $y(t)=13\\cos t$, $-\\pi/2\\le t\\le\\pi/2$. Give an exact answer.\n\n$s=$ ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405104 1071 "Source question: 8.\n\n**Question 8**\n\nFind the arc length for $x(t)=13\\sin t$, $y(t)=13\\cos t$, $-\\pi/2\\le t\\le\\pi/2$. Give an exact answer.\n\n$s=$ {{field-1}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405104 1074 "The speed is $\\sqrt{169\\cos^2t+169\\sin^2t}=13$. The parameter interval has length $\\pi$, so $s=13\\pi$, the length of a semicircle of radius $13$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405107 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050349 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405109 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049889 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405111 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048314 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405111 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048960 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405112 1068 #edb/ref 17592186405904 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405112 1068 #edb/ref 17592186405905 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405112 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406048 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405112 1071 "Source question: 5.\n\nFind the upward-pointing unit normal $\\mathbf n=n_1\\mathbf i+n_2\\mathbf j+n_3\\mathbf k$ to $z=\\sqrt{49-x^2-y^2}$, $0\\le x^2+y^2\\le49$.\n\n$n_1=$ ____.\n\n$n_2=$ ____.\n\n$n_3=$ ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405112 1071 "Source question: 5.\n\nFind the upward-pointing unit normal $\\mathbf n=n_1\\mathbf i+n_2\\mathbf j+n_3\\mathbf k$ to $z=\\sqrt{49-x^2-y^2}$, $0\\le x^2+y^2\\le49$.\n\n$n_1=$ {{n1}}.\n\n$n_2=$ {{n2}}.\n\n$n_3=$ {{n3}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405112 1074 "This is the upper radius-7 hemisphere. The radial normal (x,y,z) has length 7, so divide by 7. Its z component is positive in the interior. At the equator it extends continuously as a horizontal unit normal; a strictly upward component there is impossible, and the graph derivatives are singular." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405120 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048923 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405120 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050418 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405121 1068 #edb/ref 17592186405872 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405121 1071 "Source question: 12.\n\nLet $\\mathbf F=\\frac1\\rho\\langle5x,5y,5z\\rangle$, where $\\rho=\\sqrt{x^2+y^2+z^2}$. Calculate $\\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf F$ in terms of ρ.\n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405121 1071 "Source question: 12.\n\nLet $\\mathbf F=\\frac1\\rho\\langle5x,5y,5z\\rangle$, where $\\rho=\\sqrt{x^2+y^2+z^2}$. Calculate $\\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf F$ in terms of ρ.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405121 1074 "For ρ>0, ∂(5x/ρ)/∂x=5/ρ−5x²/ρ³, and similarly for y and z. Add to get 15/ρ−5(x²+y²+z²)/ρ³=10/ρ. The field and its divergence are undefined at the origin." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405123 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406079 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405123 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406195 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405123 1071 "**(b)** $\\phi=\\tan^{-1}(y/x)$. Find its gradient field and the points excluded from its domain.\n\nGradient: ____.\n\nExcluded points: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405123 1071 "**(b)** $\\phi=\\tan^{-1}(y/x)$. Find its gradient field and the points excluded from its domain.\n\nGradient: {{gradient}}.\n\nExcluded points: {{excluded}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405123 1074 "The chain rule gives $\\phi_x=(-y/x^2)/(1+y^2/x^2)$ and $\\phi_y=(1/x)/(1+y^2/x^2)$. Simplification gives the displayed gradient, but the original potential requires x≠0. Do not enlarge its domain just because the simplified formula exists elsewhere." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405125 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406315 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405125 1071 "Source question: 10.\n\nFind the outward electric flux of $\\mathbf E=\\langle x,2y,10z\\rangle$ across the cylindrical side $x^2+y^2=9$, $0\\le z\\le2$.\n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405125 1071 "Source question: 10.\n\nFind the outward electric flux of $\\mathbf E=\\langle x,2y,10z\\rangle$ across the cylindrical side $x^2+y^2=9$, $0\\le z\\le2$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405125 1074 "Use (3cosθ,3sinθ,z). The outward vector area is (3cosθ,3sinθ,0)dθ dz. Its dot product with E is 9cos²θ+18sin²θ. Integrate θ from 0 to 2π and z from 0 to 2: $2(9\\pi+18\\pi)=54\\pi$. The vertical component contributes nothing on the side." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405126 1064 "**6.6 Surface Integrals**\n\nDue: 2026-11-19\nSource: [Macmillan Achieve](https://achieve.macmillanlearning.com/ihub/assessment/f188d950-dd73-11e0-9572-0800200c9a66/5a1db2a7-4295-4a4c-9dbb-ceb8b174a282/d49c94ff-8be8-4c47-a468-0e086815d133)" 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405126 1064 "**6.6 Surface Integrals**\n\nDue: 2026-11-19\nSource: [Macmillan Achieve](https://achieve.macmillanlearning.com/ihub/assessment/f188d950-dd73-11e0-9572-0800200c9a66/5a1db2a7-4295-4a4c-9dbb-ceb8b174a282/d49c94ff-8be8-4c47-a468-0e086815d133)\n\nStudy answers and explanations are authored additions, not publisher solutions.\n\n## Instructions\n\nUse exact symbolic notation and fractions as needed. Problems 8–9 omit the numerical constant density; study references use ρ₀ and explain unit density. Outward normals on their open paraboloids are interpreted relative to the naturally capped solids. Source: Sullivan 2e Calculus, W.H. Freeman. Unlimited attempts, no incorrect-attempt penalty; up to 50 days late at a 2% daily penalty." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405128 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048225 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405128 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049050 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405129 1064 "**6.4 Green’s Theorem**\n\nDue: 2026-10-29\nSource: [Macmillan Achieve](https://achieve.macmillanlearning.com/ihub/assessment/f188d950-dd73-11e0-9572-0800200c9a66/5a1db2a7-4295-4a4c-9dbb-ceb8b174a282/acfcfc0c-cb8c-4110-b65a-55fe3f15159f)" 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405129 1064 "**6.4 Green’s Theorem**\n\nDue: 2026-10-29\nSource: [Macmillan Achieve](https://achieve.macmillanlearning.com/ihub/assessment/f188d950-dd73-11e0-9572-0800200c9a66/5a1db2a7-4295-4a4c-9dbb-ceb8b174a282/acfcfc0c-cb8c-4110-b65a-55fe3f15159f)\n\nStudy answers and explanations are authored additions, not publisher solutions.\n\n## Instructions\n\nGive exact answers using symbolic notation and fractions. Use Green’s theorem where required. For Problem 11, the reference answer uses the standard positive orientation of a region’s boundary; the platform prompt does not state it explicitly. Source: Sullivan 2e Calculus, W.H. Freeman. Unlimited attempts, no incorrect-attempt penalty; up to 50 days late at a 2% daily penalty." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405130 1068 #edb/ref 17592186405944 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405130 1071 "**(b)** Find $\\nabla\\eta\\cdot F$ and conclude that at every point on C, F is tangent (parallel) to C.\n\nDot product: ____. Supply the conclusion and justification below." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405130 1071 "**(b)** Find $\\nabla\\eta\\cdot F$ and conclude that at every point on C, F is tangent (parallel) to C.\n\nDot product: {{dot}}. Supply the conclusion and justification below." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405130 1074 "**conclusion:** $\\nabla\\eta\\cdot F=(2x(-y)+2yx)/\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}=0$. On C, $\\nabla\\eta$ is a nonzero normal. Thus F is perpendicular to this normal and tangent to C.\n\nUse the normal to the level curve, not the position of an arrow tip, to establish tangency. Both F and the normal are nonzero on the circle because a>0." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405131 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048225 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405131 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049050 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405133 1064 "**6.5 Divergence and Curl**\n\nDue: 2026-11-05\nSource: [Macmillan Achieve](https://achieve.macmillanlearning.com/ihub/assessment/f188d950-dd73-11e0-9572-0800200c9a66/5a1db2a7-4295-4a4c-9dbb-ceb8b174a282/c2f7ce2a-abae-4f76-b153-1b5653abd0c5)" 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405133 1064 "**6.5 Divergence and Curl**\n\nDue: 2026-11-05\nSource: [Macmillan Achieve](https://achieve.macmillanlearning.com/ihub/assessment/f188d950-dd73-11e0-9572-0800200c9a66/5a1db2a7-4295-4a4c-9dbb-ceb8b174a282/c2f7ce2a-abae-4f76-b153-1b5653abd0c5)\n\nStudy answers and explanations are authored additions, not publisher solutions.\n\n## Instructions\n\nUse exact symbolic notation and fractions as needed. Source: Sullivan 2e Calculus, W.H. Freeman. Unlimited attempts with no incorrect-attempt penalty; submissions up to 50 days late at a 2% daily penalty." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405134 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406003 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405134 1071 "**Question 1 — Part A**\n\nCompute the derivative of $f(x)=e^x\\sin(x)$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405134 1071 "**Question 1 — Part A**\n\nCompute the derivative of $f(x)=e^x\\sin(x)$.\n\n$f\\prime(x)=$ {{field-1}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405134 1074 "The product rule gives $(e^x)\\prime\\sin x+e^x(\\sin x)\\prime=e^x\\sin x+e^x\\cos x$. Both factors vary with $x$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405135 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049114 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405135 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049138 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405135 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049341 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405135 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049659 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405135 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050362 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405136 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406199 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405136 1071 "**Question 3 — Part A**\n\nEnter the rectangular equation in $x$ and $y$: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405136 1071 "**Question 3 — Part A**\n\nEnter the rectangular equation in $x$ and $y$: {{field-1}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405136 1074 "From $x=t-6$, obtain $t=x+6$. Then $y=5(x+6)+1=5x+31$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405137 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050168 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405137 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050537 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405138 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050295 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405138 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050866 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405139 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406059 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405139 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406184 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405139 1071 "Find both divergences.\n\n$\\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf F=$ ____.\n\n$\\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf G=$ ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405139 1071 "Find both divergences.\n\n$\\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf F=$ {{divf}}.\n\n$\\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf G=$ {{divg}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405139 1074 "Div F=7+3+20=30 and div G=19+5+6=30. The terms 11x and 5y in the third components vanish when differentiated with respect to z." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405141 1074 "Rewrite the lines as $y=3-3x$ and $y=(1-x)/2$. The first passes through $(0,3)$ and $(1,0)$; the second passes through $(0,1/2)$ and $(1,0)$. Plot and label both lines on the same axes. Their common point is $(1,0)$.\n\n![Reference graph showing the two lines intersecting at one point on the x-axis](images/21/212eb96c55d7bb1d6a9700e6334558773624ae385aeeb7698c16ff86b7c2f058.png)\n\nEach line has a different slope, but both reach the x-axis at $x=1$. That shared point is the intersection. Check it in both original equations: $3(1)+0=3$ and $1+2(0)=1$. Include the graph as requested; listing the point without drawing the lines leaves the graphical task unfinished." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405143 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406181 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405143 1071 "**Question 6 — Part A**\n\nEnter the rectangular equation: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405143 1071 "**Question 6 — Part A**\n\nEnter the rectangular equation: {{field-1}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405143 1074 "We have $x+3=2\\sin t$ and $y-1=2\\cos t$, so $(x+3)^2+(y-1)^2=4$. The parameter restriction gives $x\\ge-3$, selecting the right semicircle." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405144 1064 "**Math 255 — Written Homework 2**\n\nDue: unknown\nSource: [Original worksheet](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F255%2FWHW-2%2F255-WHW2.pdf)" 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405144 1064 "**Math 255 — Written Homework 2**\n\nDue: unknown\nSource: [Original worksheet](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F255%2FWHW-2%2F255-WHW2.pdf)\n\nStudy answers and explanations are authored additions, not publisher solutions.\n\n## Instructions\n\nShow clear, neat supporting work or a written explanation for every problem. Some problems are graded for correctness; logical, organized progress can earn partial credit, but correct answers without supporting work do not earn full credit. Work on the printed worksheet, digitally on the PDF, or on separate paper with the same layout. Include first name, last name, and OSU student ID on the cover. Upload a single six-page PDF to Gradescope under **Written Homework 2**: cover followed by one page per problem, including blank problems. Scan printed work or photograph separate pages; combine multiple pages for a problem into one page before merging. Digital work already follows the template. Incorrect formatting receives 0. No due date is stated in the PDF." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405145 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406002 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405145 1071 "**Question 3 — Part A**\n\nUse polar coordinates to evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{\\sqrt{3}}\\int_1^{\\sqrt{4-y^2}}\n\\sqrt{x^2+y^2-1}\\,dx\\,dy.\n$$" 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405145 1071 "**Question 3 — Part A**\n\nUse polar coordinates to evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{\\sqrt{3}}\\int_1^{\\sqrt{4-y^2}}\n\\sqrt{x^2+y^2-1}\\,dx\\,dy.\n$$\n\nIntegral: {{field-1}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405145 1074 "The region is the first-quadrant portion of $r\\le2$ with $x\\ge1$. Thus $0\\le\\theta\\le\\pi/3$ and $\\sec\\theta\\le r\\le2$. Including the Jacobian, the integral is $\\int_0^{\\pi/3}\\int_{\\sec\\theta}^2 r\\sqrt{r^2-1}\\,dr\\,d\\theta=\\frac13\\int_0^{\\pi/3}(3\\sqrt3-\\tan^3\\theta)\\,d\\theta$. Since $\\int\\tan^3\\theta\\,d\\theta=\\tfrac12\\tan^2\\theta+\\ln|\\cos\\theta|$, its value is $\\pi\\sqrt3/3-1/2+\\ln2/3$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405147 1074 "There is no point common to all $k$ lines. Their common intersection is empty.\n\nA solution must satisfy every equation at once. Two distinct parallel lines already prevent a solution. With three or more lines, each pair can intersect at a different point while no point lies on all the lines; pairwise intersection alone is insufficient." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405148 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050338 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405149 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406250 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405149 1071 "**(b)** C consists of the line segment(s) joining $(7\\pi,0)\\to(7\\pi+\\pi/2,1/2)\\to(8\\pi,1)$, in that order.\n\nIntegral: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405149 1071 "**(b)** C consists of the line segment(s) joining $(7\\pi,0)\\to(7\\pi+\\pi/2,1/2)\\to(8\\pi,1)$, in that order.\n\nIntegral: {{integral}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405149 1074 "The potential is $f=e^{2y}\\sin x$. Both endpoint values vanish, because sin(7π)=sin(8π)=0. Each path therefore has integral 0; intermediate contributions cancel." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405150 1068 #edb/ref 17592186405850 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405150 1071 "Source question: 3.\n\nFind $\\nabla\\times\\mathbf F$ for $\\mathbf F=\\langle7yz,4z^8x,4yz\\rangle$.\n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405150 1071 "Source question: 3.\n\nFind $\\nabla\\times\\mathbf F$ for $\\mathbf F=\\langle7yz,4z^8x,4yz\\rangle$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405150 1074 "The first component is R_y−Q_z=4z−32xz⁷. The second is P_z−R_x=7y−0, and the third is Q_x−P_y=4z⁸−7z." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405153 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049465 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405153 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050540 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405155 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406008 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405155 1071 "Source question: 2.\n\nFind $\\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf F$ for $\\mathbf F=\\langle5xye^z,6x^2e^z,6x^6ye^z\\rangle$.\n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405155 1071 "Source question: 2.\n\nFind $\\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf F$ for $\\mathbf F=\\langle5xye^z,6x^2e^z,6x^6ye^z\\rangle$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405155 1074 "P_x=5yeᶻ, Q_y=0 because the second component contains no y, and R_z=6x⁶yeᶻ. Add and factor; do not differentiate Q with respect to x." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405156 1074 "All $k$ lines have exactly one common point. The coordinates of that point form the unique solution.\n\nFind a point on two distinct intersecting lines, then check that every remaining line also passes through it. Coincident copies of one line do not create additional solutions once another distinct line fixes a single intersection." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405158 1068 #edb/ref 17592186405881 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405158 1071 "Source question: 1.\n\nUse the **Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals** to evaluate $\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot d\\mathbf r$. The field $\\mathbf F=\\langle6x-3y,20y-3x\\rangle$ is conservative with the supplied potential $f=3x^2-3xy+10y^2$. The piecewise smooth curve joins $(3,-1)$ to $(5,4)$.\n\nIntegral: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405158 1071 "Source question: 1.\n\nUse the **Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals** to evaluate $\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot d\\mathbf r$. The field $\\mathbf F=\\langle6x-3y,20y-3x\\rangle$ is conservative with the supplied potential $f=3x^2-3xy+10y^2$. The piecewise smooth curve joins $(3,-1)$ to $(5,4)$.\n\nIntegral: {{integral}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405158 1074 "$f(5,4)=75-60+160=175$ and $f(3,-1)=27+9+10=46$. Subtract final minus initial: 175−46=129." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405161 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406187 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405161 1071 "Source question: 3.\n\nFind $\\iint_S(x+y+z)dS$ on $z=x-20y$ above the xy-region bounded by y=0, x=1, and x=20y.\n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405161 1071 "Source question: 3.\n\nFind $\\iint_S(x+y+z)dS$ on $z=x-20y$ above the xy-region bounded by y=0, x=1, and x=20y.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405161 1074 "The region is 0≤x≤1, 0≤y≤x/20. On S, x+y+z=2x−19y and dS=√402 dxdy. The inner integral is $[2xy-19y^2/2]_0^{x/20}=61x^2/800$. Integrating x gives 61/2400." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405162 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050149 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405162 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050418 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405162 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050891 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405163 1074 "**tangent-and-orthogonal:** Tangent: $(0,-1)$. Orthogonal: no points.\n\nA tangent is $T=(1,2x)$. The determinant of F and T is 2x, so tangency requires x=0. But $F\\cdot T=1$, so orthogonality is impossible." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405164 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049946 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405164 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050142 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405165 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406274 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405165 1071 "**Question 4 — Part B**\n\nFind a general solution of the differential equation $xy'=9y-6x$.\n\nYour “general solution” should be valid at every point in an open rectangle that includes the initial condition $(x,y)=(1,-4)$.\n\nUse $C$ for the arbitrary constant." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405165 1071 "**Question 4 — Part B**\n\nFind a general solution of the differential equation $xy'=9y-6x$.\n\nYour “general solution” should be valid at every point in an open rectangle that includes the initial condition $(x,y)=(1,-4)$.\n\nUse $C$ for the arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=$ {{field-1}}, on $x>0$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405165 1074 "The product equation is $(x^{-9}y)\\prime=-6x^{-9}$. Integrating gives $x^{-9}y=(3/4)x^{-8}+C$, hence $y=(3/4)x+Cx^9$. The normalized coefficients are continuous on any open rectangle containing $(1,-4)$ whose $x$-range stays positive." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405166 1074 "**tangent-and-orthogonal:** Tangent: $(0,1)$. Orthogonal: $(1,2)$ and $(-1,2)$.\n\nOn the curve, $F=(x^2+1,-x)$ and $T=(1,2x)$. Their determinant is $x(2x^2+3)$, which vanishes only at x=0. Their dot product is $1-x^2$, so orthogonality occurs at x=±1, y=2." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405169 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048225 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405169 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050488 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405170 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406157 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405170 1071 "Source question: 7.\n\nFind the mass of the conical lamina $z=\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}$, $2\\le z\\le5$, with surface density $\\rho=30-z$.\n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405170 1071 "Source question: 7.\n\nFind the mass of the conical lamina $z=\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}$, $2\\le z\\le5$, with surface density $\\rho=30-z$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405170 1074 "Use (r cosθ,r sinθ,r), 2≤r≤5. The surface element is √2 r drdθ. Therefore $M=2\\pi\\sqrt2\\int_2^5(30-r)rdr=2\\pi\\sqrt2[15r^2-r^3/3]_2^5=552\\pi\\sqrt2$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405171 1074 "Draw the horizontal line $y=1/2$ and label it “not an integral curve.” Its slope is $0$, whereas the field has slope $2x$ there. For example, at $(1/2,1/2)$ the field slope is $1$, so the line crosses the field directions rather than following them.\n\n![Authored reference graph](images/9d/9d76186dcece593fb5232a41983bd98bbf55dde24b49d6af5430171ceb90c249.png)\n\nAn isolated point of tangency is insufficient. An integral curve must follow the field throughout its regular portions. Check the tangent mismatch at a specific point." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405172 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048923 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405172 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048960 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405172 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050994 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405174 1068 #edb/ref 17592186405849 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405174 1071 "**(a)** C consists of the line segment(s) joining $(8,\\pi/3)\\to(9,\\pi/3)\\to(9,\\pi)$, in that order.\n\nIntegral: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405174 1071 "**(a)** C consists of the line segment(s) joining $(8,\\pi/3)\\to(9,\\pi/3)\\to(9,\\pi)$, in that order.\n\nIntegral: {{integral}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405174 1074 "The potential is $f=e^x\\cos y$. All three paths have the same endpoints. Thus $f(9,\\pi)-f(8,\\pi/3)=-e^9-e^8/2$. Differentiating f verifies the two coefficients of the differential." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405175 1064 "**6.8 The Divergence Theorem**\n\nDue: 2026-12-03\nSource: [Macmillan Achieve](https://achieve.macmillanlearning.com/ihub/assessment/f188d950-dd73-11e0-9572-0800200c9a66/5a1db2a7-4295-4a4c-9dbb-ceb8b174a282/3929e7f7-d086-490c-9ebd-e2b4a59f88cf)" 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405175 1064 "**6.8 The Divergence Theorem**\n\nDue: 2026-12-03\nSource: [Macmillan Achieve](https://achieve.macmillanlearning.com/ihub/assessment/f188d950-dd73-11e0-9572-0800200c9a66/5a1db2a7-4295-4a4c-9dbb-ceb8b174a282/3929e7f7-d086-490c-9ebd-e2b4a59f88cf)\n\nStudy answers and explanations are authored additions, not publisher solutions.\n\n## Instructions\n\nUse exact symbolic notation and fractions where needed. For flux problems, n is the outer unit normal and S is the entire boundary unless stated otherwise. Use the requested theorem or formula and show its application. Source: Sullivan 2e Calculus, W.H. Freeman. Unlimited attempts, no incorrect-attempt penalty; up to 50 days late at a 2% daily penalty." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405176 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406114 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405176 1071 "**Question 5 — Part B**\n\nUse Part A to find $\\partial z/\\partial x$ for the sphere $x^2+y^2+z^2=1$.\n\n$\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}=$ ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405176 1071 "**Question 5 — Part B**\n\nUse Part A to find $\\partial z/\\partial x$ for the sphere $x^2+y^2+z^2=1$.\n\n$\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}=$ {{field-1}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405176 1074 "For $f=x^2+y^2+z^2$, the partial derivatives are $f_x=2x$ and $f_z=2z$. Holding $y$ fixed gives $z_x=-2x/(2z)=-x/z$ for $z\\ne0$. The term $y^2$ has zero partial derivative with respect to $x$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405177 1064 "**1.1 Parametric Equations**\n\nSource: [Macmillan Achieve](https://achieve.macmillanlearning.com/ihub/assessment/f188d950-dd73-11e0-9572-0800200c9a66/5a1db2a7-4295-4a4c-9dbb-ceb8b174a282/1af7ea5c-56c7-4f8d-aaec-e540bb4b3c9e?actualCourseId=5a1db2a7-4295-4a4c-9dbb-ceb8b174a282)\nDue: 2026-10-01" 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405177 1064 "**1.1 Parametric Equations**\n\nSource: [Macmillan Achieve](https://achieve.macmillanlearning.com/ihub/assessment/f188d950-dd73-11e0-9572-0800200c9a66/5a1db2a7-4295-4a4c-9dbb-ceb8b174a282/1af7ea5c-56c7-4f8d-aaec-e540bb4b3c9e?actualCourseId=5a1db2a7-4295-4a4c-9dbb-ceb8b174a282)\nDue: 2026-10-01\nImported: 2026-10-01 · 6 questions\n\nStudy answers, explanations, and reference graphics are authored additions to the linked source." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405179 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406300 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405179 1071 "Source question: 3.\n\nFind the divergence of $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=\\langle7x+4\\cos x,8y+7y\\sin x,7z\\rangle$. \n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405179 1071 "Source question: 3.\n\nFind the divergence of $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=\\langle7x+4\\cos x,8y+7y\\sin x,7z\\rangle$. \n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405179 1074 "$P_x=7-4\\sin x$, $Q_y=8+7\\sin x$, $R_z=7$. Their sum is 22+3sin x." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405180 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406235 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405180 1071 "Source question: 1.\n\nFind $\\nabla\\times\\mathbf F$ for $\\mathbf F=\\langle4x,2y,0\\rangle$.\n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405180 1071 "Source question: 1.\n\nFind $\\nabla\\times\\mathbf F$ for $\\mathbf F=\\langle4x,2y,0\\rangle$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405180 1074 "For F=(P,Q,R), curl F=(R_y−Q_z,P_z−R_x,Q_x−P_y). All six cross partials here vanish." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405181 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406067 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405181 1071 "**(a)** $\\mathbf F=\\langle x,y\\rangle=x\\mathbf i+y\\mathbf j$.\n\nPolar form: ____. Explain how the result agrees with the field plot." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405181 1071 "**(a)** $\\mathbf F=\\langle x,y\\rangle=x\\mathbf i+y\\mathbf j$.\n\nPolar form: {{polar}}. Explain how the result agrees with the field plot." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405181 1074 "**interpretation:** The radial coefficient is $x\\cos\\theta+y\\sin\\theta=r$, and the angular coefficient is $-x\\sin\\theta+y\\cos\\theta=0$. It points outward with length r.\n\nThe radial coefficient is $x\\cos\\theta+y\\sin\\theta=r$, and the angular coefficient is $-x\\sin\\theta+y\\cos\\theta=0$. It points outward with length r. Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$ and the basis identities from 4(b). Polar directions require r>0; in (a) and (c), the Cartesian field extends to the zero vector at the origin." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405182 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048575 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405182 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049137 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405182 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049412 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405182 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049780 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405183 1068 #edb/ref 17592186405855 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405183 1071 "Source question: 18.\n\nFind the work of $\\mathbf F=\\langle8xy,12y^2\\rangle$ along $\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t,t^2\\rangle$ from t=0 to t=2.\n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405183 1071 "Source question: 18.\n\nFind the work of $\\mathbf F=\\langle8xy,12y^2\\rangle$ along $\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t,t^2\\rangle$ from t=0 to t=2.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405183 1074 "$F(r)=(8t^3,12t^4)$ and $r\\prime=(1,2t)$. Integrate $8t^3+24t^5$: $[2t^4+4t^6]_0^2=32+256$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405185 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406302 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405185 1071 "Source question: 8.\n\nUse **Green’s theorem** to find the area bounded by $y=1/\\sqrt{x-7}$, y=0, x=10, and x=18.\n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405185 1071 "Source question: 8.\n\nUse **Green’s theorem** to find the area bounded by $y=1/\\sqrt{x-7}$, y=0, x=10, and x=18.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405185 1074 "Choose P=−y,Q=0, giving $Q_x-P_y=1$ and $A=-\\oint_Cy\\,dx$ for positive orientation. Only the upper boundary contributes; it is traversed from x=18 to 10. Hence $A=\\int_{10}^{18}(x-7)^{-1/2}dx=2(\\sqrt{11}-\\sqrt3)$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405187 1074 "Since $c$ is constant and $y=y(x)$, the chain rule gives $0=f_x\\,dx/dx+f_y\\,dy/dx=f_x+f_y\\,dy/dx$. If $f_y\\ne0$, solving gives $dy/dx=-f_x/f_y$.\n\nBoth paths in the dependence tree contribute. The direct $x$ path gives $f_x$ because $dx/dx=1$; the $y$ path gives $f_y\\,dy/dx$. Division by $f_y$ requires $f_y\\ne0$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405188 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406301 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405188 1071 "**Question 6 — Part B**\n\nChoose the graph of the curve over the stated parameter interval." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405188 1071 "**Question 6 — Part B**\n\nChoose the graph of the curve over the stated parameter interval.\n\n1. ![Choice 1](images/d8/d8d313e6992bdcee73117a6545d58863158f954e7cf02323b354d7dc063246db.png)\n\n2. ![Choice 2](images/3d/3d57f723a301e24bca20d2afba11c916dc64a227f01b2b3e0c1303fb970dd09d.png)\n\n3. ![Choice 3](images/0d/0de918c52f40b809ab0ecc0f82a21a5abbb915759e660deec65b6c8985ec05a9.png)\n\n4. ![Choice 4](images/60/6079126817353a92c6a26aece71b4a9119b24f0c1fa34d8b5c201adb32208edc.png)" 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405188 1074 "The center is $(-3,1)$ and the radius is $2$. Since $\\sin t\\ge0$ on $[0,\\pi]$, the curve is the right semicircle, from $(-3,3)$ through $(-1,1)$ to $(-3,-1)$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405189 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050295 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405189 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050866 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405191 1089 #edb/ref 17592186051091 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405193 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048299 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405193 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048819 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405193 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050866 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405194 1074 "The base area is $\\lVert\\mathbf u\\times\\mathbf v\\rVert$ and the height is $\\lVert\\mathbf w\\rVert|\\cos\\theta|$. Their product is $|\\lVert\\mathbf w\\rVert\\lVert\\mathbf u\\times\\mathbf v\\rVert\\cos\\theta|=|\\mathbf w\\cdot(\\mathbf u\\times\\mathbf v)|$.\n\nThe dot product measures the component of $\\mathbf w$ along the base normal, multiplied by the base area. Its sign records orientation; absolute value gives nonnegative volume. A degenerate base or a side lying in the base plane gives zero volume." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405196 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406057 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405196 1071 "Source question: 1.\n\nLet $S:z=4x+3y+5$, $0\\le x\\le1$, $0\\le y\\le4$. Find $\\iint_S(x^2+y^2)dS$.\n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405196 1071 "Source question: 1.\n\nLet $S:z=4x+3y+5$, $0\\le x\\le1$, $0\\le y\\le4$. Find $\\iint_S(x^2+y^2)dS$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405196 1074 "$dS=\\sqrt{1+4^2+3^2}dxdy=\\sqrt{26}dxdy$. Over the rectangle, the integral of x² is 4/3 and that of y² is 64/3. Their sum is 68/3." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405197 1074 "Plot $y=(1-x)/3$ using, for example, $(0,1/3)$ and $(1,0)$. Plot $y=4x-3$ using $(0,-3)$ and $(3/4,0)$. Draw both lines on the same axes and mark their common point.\n\nThe graph places the intersection near $(0.77,0.08)$. Its exact coordinates are $\\left(\\frac{10}{13},\\frac{1}{13}\\right)$.\n\n![Reference graph showing both lines and their intersection](images/6b/6b7c1afd7bdc97e66c0477241907e1be5bc065b6e7f456182fc68b509d2012a5.png)\n\nA solution must lie on both lines, so look for their crossing rather than either line's intercept. Your response should include two labeled lines on common axes and an intersection consistent with your plotting scale. The exact point checks both equations: $10/13+3(1/13)=1$ and $4(10/13)-1/13=3$. An algebraic answer alone does not complete the requested graph." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405198 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406284 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405198 1071 "Source question: 2.\n\nLet $S:z=7x^2+6y$, $0\\le x\\le1$, $0\\le y\\le2$. Find $\\iint_S4x\\,dS$.\n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405198 1071 "Source question: 2.\n\nLet $S:z=7x^2+6y$, $0\\le x\\le1$, $0\\le y\\le2$. Find $\\iint_S4x\\,dS$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405198 1074 "$dS=\\sqrt{37+196x^2}dxdy$. Integrating out y gives $\\int_0^1 8x\\sqrt{37+196x^2}dx$. Substitute u=37+196x²; the result is $2[u^{3/2}]_{37}^{233}/147$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405201 1074 "**proof:** Both squared norms are $\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1$. Their dot product is $-\\cos\\theta\\sin\\theta+\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta=0$. Thus each has length 1 and they are perpendicular.\n\nCheck both lengths and the dot product; orthogonality alone does not imply unit length." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405207 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050573 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405209 1068 #edb/ref 17592186405992 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405209 1071 "Source question: 9.\n\nFind the curl of $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=\\langle5\\sin(6xy),7\\cos(xy^2),7x\\rangle$. Use component form or standard basis vectors.\n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405209 1071 "Source question: 9.\n\nFind the curl of $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=\\langle5\\sin(6xy),7\\cos(xy^2),7x\\rangle$. Use component form or standard basis vectors.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405209 1074 "$R_y-Q_z=0$ and $P_z-R_x=-7$. The third component is $Q_x-P_y=-7y^2\\sin(xy^2)-30x\\cos(6xy)$ by the chain rule." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405210 1089 #edb/ref 17592186051091 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405211 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406143 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405211 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406281 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405211 1071 "Textbook problem **2.1.43**.\n\n**Question 2**\n\nExperiments indicate that glucose is absorbed by the body at a rate proportional to the amount of glucose present in the bloodstream. Let $\\lambda$ denote the (positive) constant of proportionality. Now suppose glucose is injected into a patient's bloodstream at a constant rate of $r$ units per unit of time. Let $G=G(t)$ be the number of units in the patient's bloodstream at time $t>0$. Then\n\n$$\nG'=-\\lambda G+r,\n$$\n\nwhere the first term on the right is due to the absorption of the glucose by the patient's body and the second term is due to the injection. Determine $G$ for $t>0$, given that $G(0)=G_0$. Also, find\n\n$$\n\\lim_{t\\to\\infty}G(t).\n$$" 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405211 1071 "Textbook problem **2.1.43**.\n\n**Question 2**\n\nExperiments indicate that glucose is absorbed by the body at a rate proportional to the amount of glucose present in the bloodstream. Let $\\lambda$ denote the (positive) constant of proportionality. Now suppose glucose is injected into a patient's bloodstream at a constant rate of $r$ units per unit of time. Let $G=G(t)$ be the number of units in the patient's bloodstream at time $t>0$. Then\n\n$$\nG'=-\\lambda G+r,\n$$\n\nwhere the first term on the right is due to the absorption of the glucose by the patient's body and the second term is due to the injection. Determine $G$ for $t>0$, given that $G(0)=G_0$. Also, find\n\n$$\n\\lim_{t\\to\\infty}G(t).\n$$\n\n$G(t)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\lim_{t\\to\\infty}G(t)=$ {{field-2}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405211 1074 "The integrating factor is $e^{\\lambda t}$, giving $(e^{\\lambda t}G)\\prime=re^{\\lambda t}$. Hence $G=r/\\lambda+Ce^{-\\lambda t}$, with $C=G_0-r/\\lambda$. Because $\\lambda>0$, the exponential tends to zero. Differentiation verifies $G\\prime=-\\lambda G+r$, and $G(0)=G_0$. This is a mathematical model, with equilibrium $r/\\lambda$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405213 1068 #edb/ref 17592186405886 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405213 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406138 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405213 1071 "**Question 6 — Part B**\n\nCompute both dot products. Conclude that $\\nabla\\varphi$ is perpendicular to both vectors and hence to the level surface at $(x,y,z)$.\n\n$\\nabla\\varphi\\cdot\\langle\\varphi_z,0,-\\varphi_x\\rangle=$ ____.\n\n$\\nabla\\varphi\\cdot\\langle0,\\varphi_z,-\\varphi_y\\rangle=$ ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405213 1071 "**Question 6 — Part B**\n\nCompute both dot products. Conclude that $\\nabla\\varphi$ is perpendicular to both vectors and hence to the level surface at $(x,y,z)$.\n\n$\\nabla\\varphi\\cdot\\langle\\varphi_z,0,-\\varphi_x\\rangle=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\nabla\\varphi\\cdot\\langle0,\\varphi_z,-\\varphi_y\\rangle=$ {{field-2}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405213 1074 "For $\\nabla\\varphi=\\langle\\varphi_x,\\varphi_y,\\varphi_z\\rangle$, the products are $\\varphi_x\\varphi_z-\\varphi_z\\varphi_x=0$ and $\\varphi_y\\varphi_z-\\varphi_z\\varphi_y=0$. When $\\varphi_z\\ne0$, Part A supplies two independent tangent directions, so orthogonality to both makes the gradient normal to the tangent plane. Include this conclusion, not just the two zeros." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405214 1068 #edb/ref 17592186405910 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405214 1071 "[Open in WeBWorK](https://webwork2.science.oregonstate.edu/webwork2/MTH_256_020_F2026/WebWork_1/5)\n\n**Question 5**\n\nSolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\ny'+\\frac{1}{x+7}y=x^{-2},\\qquad y(1)=6.\n$$" 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405214 1071 "[Open in WeBWorK](https://webwork2.science.oregonstate.edu/webwork2/MTH_256_020_F2026/WebWork_1/5)\n\n**Question 5**\n\nSolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\ny'+\\frac{1}{x+7}y=x^{-2},\\qquad y(1)=6.\n$$\n\n$y(x)=$ {{field-1}}, on $x>0$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405214 1074 "An integrating factor on the interval containing $1$ is $x+7$. Thus $[(x+7)y]\\prime=(x+7)/x^2=1/x+7/x^2$, and $(x+7)y=\\ln x-7/x+C$. At $x=1$, $48=-7+C$, so $C=55$. The maximal coefficient interval containing the initial point is $(0,\\infty)$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405216 1089 #edb/ref 17592186051091 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405219 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406310 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405219 1071 "Source question: 4.\n\nFind $\\int_C7y\\,ds$ along $y=x^3/2$ from (0,0) to (2,4).\n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405219 1071 "Source question: 4.\n\nFind $\\int_C7y\\,ds$ along $y=x^3/2$ from (0,0) to (2,4).\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405219 1074 "$ds=\\sqrt{1+9x^4/4}\\,dx$ and 7y=7x³/2. Set u=1+9x⁴/4, du=9x³dx. This gives $\\frac7{27}[u^{3/2}]_1^{37}$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405222 1068 #edb/ref 17592186405900 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405222 1071 "**(c)** C consists of the line segment(s) joining $(8,\\pi/3)\\to(8,\\pi/2)\\to(9,\\pi)$, in that order.\n\nIntegral: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405222 1071 "**(c)** C consists of the line segment(s) joining $(8,\\pi/3)\\to(8,\\pi/2)\\to(9,\\pi)$, in that order.\n\nIntegral: {{integral}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405222 1074 "The potential is $f=e^x\\cos y$. All three paths have the same endpoints. Thus $f(9,\\pi)-f(8,\\pi/3)=-e^9-e^8/2$. Differentiating f verifies the two coefficients of the differential." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405224 1068 #edb/ref 17592186405993 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405224 1071 "**Question 1 — Part B**\n\nWhat is the maximum rate of change of the voltage at $P$?" 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405224 1071 "**Question 1 — Part B**\n\nWhat is the maximum rate of change of the voltage at $P$?\n\nMaximum rate: {{field-1}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405224 1074 "The largest directional derivative is $\\|\\nabla V(P)\\|=\\sqrt{12^2+0^2+16^2}=20$, by Cauchy–Schwarz for unit directions." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405225 1074 "By the chain rule, $dw/dt=2x(-\\sin t)+2y\\cos t+2z=2\\cos t(-\\sin t)+2\\sin t\\cos t+2t=2t$. This makes sense because on the helix $w=1+t^2$: the circular motion leaves $x^2+y^2$ unchanged while height changes.\n\nInclude all three coordinate contributions. Substituting into $w$ first gives an independent check, but the requested derivation should explicitly use the chain rule." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405226 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049768 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405226 1089 #edb/ref 17592186051091 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405227 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050989 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405228 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048832 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405228 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049150 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405228 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049168 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405230 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050573 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405232 1089 #edb/ref 17592186051076 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405235 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050514 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405236 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406178 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405236 1071 "Source question: 10.\n\nLet $\\mathbf F=\\langle x,y,z\\rangle$ and use $V=\\frac13\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,dS$, with outward n, to find the volume of a sphere of radius R.\n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405236 1071 "Source question: 10.\n\nLet $\\mathbf F=\\langle x,y,z\\rangle$ and use $V=\\frac13\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,dS$, with outward n, to find the volume of a sphere of radius R.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405236 1074 "Center the sphere at the origin. On its surface n=(x,y,z)/R, so F·n=R. Multiplying by its surface area 4πR² gives flux 4πR³. Divide by 3 to obtain V=4πR³/3." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405237 1068 #edb/ref 17592186405938 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405237 1071 "Source question: 9.\n\nLet $\\mathbf F=\\langle x,y,z\\rangle$ and use $V=\\frac13\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,dS$, with outward n, to find the volume of a right circular cone of height h and base radius R. Hint: orient the cone with vertex at the origin as shown.\n\n![Cone with vertex at the origin](images/b6/b680c204ce20d30c0451dede0c53b8fbef270bbacb3a2bcc6084796cb91a3018.png)\n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405237 1071 "Source question: 9.\n\nLet $\\mathbf F=\\langle x,y,z\\rangle$ and use $V=\\frac13\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,dS$, with outward n, to find the volume of a right circular cone of height h and base radius R. Hint: orient the cone with vertex at the origin as shown.\n\n![Cone with vertex at the origin](images/b6/b680c204ce20d30c0451dede0c53b8fbef270bbacb3a2bcc6084796cb91a3018.png)\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405237 1074 "Place the vertex at the origin and base at z=h. The position vector F is tangent to the conical side along each generating line, so the side contributes zero flux. On the top disk the outward normal is k, so F·n=h and flux=hπR². The stated formula gives V=πR²h/3." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405238 1074 "Where $\\varphi_y\\ne0$, a tangent direction is $\\langle1,dy/dx\\rangle=\\langle1,-\\varphi_x/\\varphi_y\\rangle$. Multiplying by $\\varphi_y$ gives $\\langle\\varphi_y,-\\varphi_x\\rangle$, so it is tangent. If $\\varphi_y=0$ but $\\varphi_x\\ne0$, solving locally for $x(y)$ instead gives a vertical tangent, also represented by this vector.\n\nMultiplying a nonzero tangent vector by a nonzero scalar preserves its tangent line, even if the direction reverses. A meaningful tangent direction requires a regular point: $\\nabla\\varphi\\ne\\mathbf0$. At a critical point this formula may give the zero vector and does not determine a tangent." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405239 1064 "Source: [MTH341-WHW1.pdf](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F341%2FWHW-1%2FMTH341-WHW1.pdf)\nDue: 2026-10-07\n\nTextbook: [A First Course in Linear Algebra — Version 2017, Revision A](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F341%2FKuttler-LinearAlgebra-AFirstCourse-2017A.pdf), by Lyryx Learning, based on the original text by K. Kuttler. Exercise text reproduced from the CC BY textbook; see its license page." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405239 1064 "Source: [MTH341-WHW1.pdf](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F341%2FWHW-1%2FMTH341-WHW1.pdf)\nDue: 2026-10-07\n\nTextbook: [A First Course in Linear Algebra — Version 2017, Revision A](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F341%2FKuttler-LinearAlgebra-AFirstCourse-2017A.pdf), by Lyryx Learning, based on the original text by K. Kuttler. Exercise text reproduced from the CC BY textbook; see its license page.\n\nStudy answers, explanations, and reference graphs are authored additions to the linked textbook exercises.\n\n## Instructions\n\nPut your solutions on separate sheets of paper (8.5 × 11 inches or A4). Use the original assignment sheet as a cover sheet and fill in your name and student ID. Submit your homework on Canvas. The assignment sheet states that late homework is not accepted." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405240 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049512 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405240 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050037 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405240 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050796 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405240 1089 #edb/ref 17592186051090 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405242 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048239 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405242 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048641 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405242 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049261 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405242 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050037 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405242 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050149 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405245 1064 "**1.2 Calculus of Parametric Curves**\n\nSource: [Macmillan Achieve](https://achieve.macmillanlearning.com/ihub/assessment/f188d950-dd73-11e0-9572-0800200c9a66/5a1db2a7-4295-4a4c-9dbb-ceb8b174a282/2800fbc5-4e58-452a-89b1-afe688a3b8bf?actualCourseId=5a1db2a7-4295-4a4c-9dbb-ceb8b174a282)\nDue: 2026-10-01" 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405245 1064 "**1.2 Calculus of Parametric Curves**\n\nSource: [Macmillan Achieve](https://achieve.macmillanlearning.com/ihub/assessment/f188d950-dd73-11e0-9572-0800200c9a66/5a1db2a7-4295-4a4c-9dbb-ceb8b174a282/2800fbc5-4e58-452a-89b1-afe688a3b8bf?actualCourseId=5a1db2a7-4295-4a4c-9dbb-ceb8b174a282)\nDue: 2026-10-01\nImported: 2026-10-01 · 10 questions\n\nStudy answers, explanations, and reference graphics are authored additions to the linked source." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405249 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406117 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405249 1071 "Source question: 5.\n\nFor the conservative field $\\mathbf F=\\langle7x^2+y^2,2xy+\\sin y\\rangle$, evaluate $\\int_C(7x^2+y^2)dx+(2xy+\\sin y)dy$ from (0,0) to (4,0) along a piecewise smooth curve.\n\nIntegral: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405249 1071 "Source question: 5.\n\nFor the conservative field $\\mathbf F=\\langle7x^2+y^2,2xy+\\sin y\\rangle$, evaluate $\\int_C(7x^2+y^2)dx+(2xy+\\sin y)dy$ from (0,0) to (4,0) along a piecewise smooth curve.\n\nIntegral: {{integral}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405249 1074 "A potential is $f=7x^3/3+xy^2-\\cos y$. Its partial derivatives reproduce the field. Then $f(4,0)-f(0,0)=(448/3-1)-(-1)=448/3$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405251 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050566 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405253 1074 "$w_r=2x\\cos\\theta+2y\\sin\\theta+2z=4r$. Also $w_\\theta=2x(-r\\sin\\theta)+2y(r\\cos\\theta)+2z(0)=0$. On the cone $w=2r^2$, so it increases with radius and is independent of the angular coordinate.\n\nFor $w_r$, the $z=r$ coordinate contributes another $2r$. For $w_\\theta$, the sine/cosine terms cancel. Hold the other parameter fixed for each partial derivative." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405256 1064 "MTH 256 F 2026 Written Homework 1\n\nDate imported: 2026-09-30\nDue: 2026-10-02\n\nSource: [Original worksheet](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F256%2FWHW-1%2F256-WHW1.pdf)\n\nStudy answers, explanations, and reference graphics are authored additions to the linked source." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405256 1064 "MTH 256 F 2026 Written Homework 1\n\nDue: 2026-10-02\n\nSource: [Original worksheet](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F256%2FWHW-1%2F256-WHW1.pdf)" 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405258 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048960 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405258 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049721 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405259 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406111 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405259 1071 "Source question: 11.\n\nFind c so that $\\mathbf F=\\langle9xy,5cx^2,0\\rangle$ is conservative in space.\n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405259 1071 "Source question: 11.\n\nFind c so that $\\mathbf F=\\langle9xy,5cx^2,0\\rangle$ is conservative in space.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405259 1074 "The only nontrivial cross-partial condition is P_y=Q_x: 9x=10cx for every x. Hence c=9/10. A global potential is f=(9/2)x²y, independent of z, which verifies sufficiency." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405261 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406103 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405261 1071 "**Question 2 — Part B**\n\nGive the domain in interval notation: $x\\in$ ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405261 1071 "**Question 2 — Part B**\n\nGive the domain in interval notation: $x\\in$ {{field-1}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405261 1074 "As $t$ ranges over all real numbers, the nonconstant linear function $x=8t+1$ also takes every real value. The domain is $(-\\infty,\\infty)$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405263 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406159 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405263 1071 "**Question 11 — Part B**\n\nHow does the magnitude of a field vector depend on its position?" 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405263 1071 "**Question 11 — Part B**\n\nHow does the magnitude of a field vector depend on its position?\n\n1. It equals the radius of the circle.\n\n2. It is proportional to the distance from the $y$-axis.\n\n3. It equals the square of the circle radius.\n\n4. It is proportional to the distance from the $x$-axis." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405263 1074 "The magnitude is $\\sqrt{(-y)^2+x^2}=\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}$, which is the radius of the circle centered at the origin." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405264 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406207 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405264 1071 "**Question 4 — Part B**\n\nPoints with vertical tangents: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405264 1071 "**Question 4 — Part B**\n\nPoints with vertical tangents: {{field-1}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405264 1074 "A vertical tangent requires $x'=0$ and $y'\\ne0$. Solving $3t^2-144=0$ gives $t=\\pm4\\sqrt3$. Since $x=t(48-144)=-96t$ and $y=96$, the points are $(-384\\sqrt3,96)$ and $(384\\sqrt3,96)$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405265 1089 #edb/ref 17592186051091 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405266 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406123 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405266 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406173 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405266 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406221 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405266 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406246 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405266 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406270 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405266 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406304 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405266 1071 "Source question: 2, PDF page 3.\n\nLet $\\mathbf F=\\langle x,y\\rangle$, $\\mathbf G=\\langle-y,x\\rangle$, and $\\mathbf H=\\langle x,-y\\rangle$. Match plots A–F to (a)–(f). Some plots are scaled for clarity.\n\n![Vector field plots A–F](images/ba/bab4c1e1ab7751751726e766e5fadef2c28e3dce5665f44345eb3315c65355cd.png)\n\n(a) $-\\mathbf G$; (b) $\\mathbf G-\\mathbf F$; (c) $\\mathbf F+\\mathbf H$; (d) $\\mathbf G+\\mathbf H$; (e) $\\mathbf H$; (f) $\\mathbf G+\\mathbf F$.\n\nPlot A: ____.\n\nPlot B: ____.\n\nPlot C: ____.\n\nPlot D: ____.\n\nPlot E: ____.\n\nPlot F: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405266 1071 "Source question: 2, PDF page 3.\n\nLet $\\mathbf F=\\langle x,y\\rangle$, $\\mathbf G=\\langle-y,x\\rangle$, and $\\mathbf H=\\langle x,-y\\rangle$. Match plots A–F to (a)–(f). Some plots are scaled for clarity.\n\n![Vector field plots A–F](images/ba/bab4c1e1ab7751751726e766e5fadef2c28e3dce5665f44345eb3315c65355cd.png)\n\n(a) $-\\mathbf G$; (b) $\\mathbf G-\\mathbf F$; (c) $\\mathbf F+\\mathbf H$; (d) $\\mathbf G+\\mathbf H$; (e) $\\mathbf H$; (f) $\\mathbf G+\\mathbf F$.\n\nPlot A: {{plot-a}}.\n\nPlot B: {{plot-b}}.\n\nPlot C: {{plot-c}}.\n\nPlot D: {{plot-d}}.\n\nPlot E: {{plot-e}}.\n\nPlot F: {{plot-f}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405266 1074 "A–e, B–f, C–c, D–d, E–b, F–a. Compute components: $-G=(y,-x)$ is clockwise; $G-F=(-x-y,x-y)$ rotates inward; $F+H=(2x,0)$ is horizontal; $G+H=(x-y,x-y)$ has equal components; $H=(x,-y)$ is a saddle; $G+F=(x-y,x+y)$ rotates outward. Check signs on each axis to distinguish B from E." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405267 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406242 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405267 1071 "**(b)** Evaluate $\\int_C3x\\,dx+8y\\,dy$ along a piecewise smooth curve from (1,3) to (2,5). Integral: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405267 1071 "**(b)** Evaluate $\\int_C3x\\,dx+8y\\,dy$ along a piecewise smooth curve from (1,3) to (2,5). Integral: {{integral}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405267 1074 "Use the potential from part (a). The final value (without K) is 6+100=106; the initial value is 3/2+36=75/2. Their difference is 137/2." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405269 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048314 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405269 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050875 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405270 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406249 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405270 1071 "Source question: 5.\n\n**Question 5**\n\nChoose the graph of $\\mathbf F(x,y)=4\\mathbf i$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405270 1071 "Source question: 5.\n\n**Question 5**\n\nChoose the graph of $\\mathbf F(x,y)=4\\mathbf i$.\n\n1. ![Choice 1](images/8b/8ba99ec8a867882a71b560d11351f39268f3eb985d51e48523567c3674223326.png)\n\n2. ![Choice 2](images/56/563c378cec5487c565832aba16b8bfcb1d8478bd78a7248bb61197d7adef9e45.png)\n\n3. ![Choice 3](images/94/9470bca6690cabedcbd27ae98bf7c4083f762dad0187d886ba6a3191afa584a3.png)\n\n4. ![Choice 4](images/dd/ddfe7805e9be508eef77dbf5defac2150d9aeeac96fd0d093dcf4ffe2effd261.png)" 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405270 1074 "The vector $4\\mathbf i=\\langle4,0\\rangle$ is independent of position. Every arrow has the same length and points right." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405272 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049492 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405272 1089 #edb/ref 17592186051090 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405273 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048960 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405273 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049859 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405278 1068 #edb/ref 17592186405970 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405278 1071 "Source question: 3.\n\nFind $\\int_C3x\\,ds$ for $x=3t,\\ y=7t^2,\\ 0\\le t\\le1$.\n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405278 1071 "Source question: 3.\n\nFind $\\int_C3x\\,ds$ for $x=3t,\\ y=7t^2,\\ 0\\le t\\le1$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405278 1074 "$ds=\\sqrt{9+196t^2}dt$. Integrate $9t\\sqrt{9+196t^2}$ using u=9+196t², du=392t dt; the antiderivative is $3(9+196t^2)^{3/2}/196$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405279 1068 #edb/ref 17592186405880 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405279 1071 "Source question: 21.\n\nFind the work of $\\mathbf F=\\langle y^3,x^3\\rangle$ along $\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle6\\cos t,8\\sin t\\rangle$ from t=0 to t=2π.\n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405279 1071 "Source question: 21.\n\nFind the work of $\\mathbf F=\\langle y^3,x^3\\rangle$ along $\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle6\\cos t,8\\sin t\\rangle$ from t=0 to t=2π.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405279 1074 "The dot product is $-3072\\sin^4t+1728\\cos^4t$. Both fourth-power integrals over 0 to 2π equal 3π/4, giving $(1728-3072)3\\pi/4=-1008\\pi$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405281 1068 #edb/ref 17592186405945 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405281 1071 "**Question 5 — Part A**\n\nPoints with horizontal tangents: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405281 1071 "**Question 5 — Part A**\n\nPoints with horizontal tangents: {{field-1}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405281 1074 "Here $x'=10\\sin t$ and $y'=-3\\cos t$. Horizontal tangents occur at $t=\\pi/2,3\\pi/2$, where $y'=0$ and $x'\\ne0$, giving $(10,0)$ and $(10,6)$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405282 1068 #edb/ref 17592186405856 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405282 1071 "**Question 1 — Part C**\n\nCompute the following derivative ($\\exp(x)=e^x$):\n\n$$\n\\frac{d}{dx}\\exp(x^2).\n$$" 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405282 1071 "**Question 1 — Part C**\n\nCompute the following derivative ($\\exp(x)=e^x$):\n\n$$\n\\frac{d}{dx}\\exp(x^2).\n$$\n\nDerivative: {{field-1}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405282 1074 "For $e^{u(x)}$, the derivative is $e^{u(x)}u\\prime(x)$. Here $u=x^2$ and $u\\prime=2x$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405284 1068 #edb/ref 17592186405857 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405284 1071 "Source question: 6.\n\n**Question 6**\n\nChoose the graph of $\\mathbf F(x,y)=-2\\mathbf i+3\\mathbf j$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405284 1071 "Source question: 6.\n\n**Question 6**\n\nChoose the graph of $\\mathbf F(x,y)=-2\\mathbf i+3\\mathbf j$.\n\n1. ![Choice 1](images/8e/8ea9e162cfb077650d20a62d85861c1ef12532b1a804e41b284d96ee7c4169cb.png)\n\n2. ![Choice 2](images/e6/e68cee6eb0f073b11a3b3f148e29e5d3b7a0a320a13fe9f8764fe8d82ea97cac.png)\n\n3. ![Choice 3](images/4d/4d441ffabd4b12401092f6e187ad5fd91bb58182033a09fe32160ce5559bf18b.png)\n\n4. ![Choice 4](images/cd/cdb064db227c4a73338ac560c3e45c971263e8807fad6b340ec9f9366cbdf214.png)" 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405284 1074 "The constant vector $\\langle-2,3\\rangle$ points left and up, with vertical magnitude larger than horizontal magnitude. All arrows agree in direction and length." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405285 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048575 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405285 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049487 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405285 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050614 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405286 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048314 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405286 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050149 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405286 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050875 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405290 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050573 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405291 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406240 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405291 1071 "Source question: 23.\n\nFind the work of $\\mathbf F=\\langle44x,6y,3\\rangle$ along $\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle\\sin t,\\cos t,t\\rangle$, $0\\le t\\le\\pi$.\n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405291 1071 "Source question: 23.\n\nFind the work of $\\mathbf F=\\langle44x,6y,3\\rangle$ along $\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle\\sin t,\\cos t,t\\rangle$, $0\\le t\\le\\pi$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405291 1074 "The dot product is $38\\sin t\\cos t+3$. The trigonometric term integrates to zero over 0 to π. Alternatively use the potential $22x^2+3y^2+3z$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405292 1068 #edb/ref 17592186405917 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405292 1071 "Source question: 3.\n\nFind $\\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf F$ for $\\mathbf F=\\langle\\sqrt{4x^2+5y^2},\\sqrt{4x^2+5y^2},2z\\rangle$.\n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405292 1071 "Source question: 3.\n\nFind $\\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf F$ for $\\mathbf F=\\langle\\sqrt{4x^2+5y^2},\\sqrt{4x^2+5y^2},2z\\rangle$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405292 1074 "The chain rule gives P_x=4x/√(4x²+5y²) and Q_y=5y/√(4x²+5y²); R_z=2. The formula applies when (x,y)≠(0,0). The required partial derivatives fail to exist on the z-axis, so the divergence is undefined there." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405293 1068 #edb/ref 17592186405842 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405293 1071 "**(a)** Find the unique two-dimensional field that is undefined at (3,2), unit everywhere else, and points toward (3,2).\n\n$\\mathbf F(x,y)=$ ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405293 1071 "**(a)** Find the unique two-dimensional field that is undefined at (3,2), unit everywhere else, and points toward (3,2).\n\n$\\mathbf F(x,y)=$ {{field}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405293 1074 "The displacement toward (3,2) is $(3-x,2-y)$. Divide by its positive length. The denominator vanishes exactly at (3,2), and the resulting magnitude is 1." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405294 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048239 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405294 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048923 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405294 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049721 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405295 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406131 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405295 1071 "Source question: 2.\n\nFind the divergence of $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=\\langle8x,3xy,6xyz^5\\rangle$. \n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405295 1071 "Source question: 2.\n\nFind the divergence of $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=\\langle8x,3xy,6xyz^5\\rangle$. \n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405295 1074 "$P_x=8$, $Q_y=3x$, $R_z=30xyz^4$. Hold the other coordinates fixed in each partial derivative." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405299 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048641 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405299 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050037 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405300 1064 "Source: [MTH255-WHW-Week1_v1.pdf](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F255%2FWHW-1%2FMTH255-WHW-Week1_v1.pdf)\nDue: 2026-10-01" 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405300 1064 "Source: [MTH255-WHW-Week1_v1.pdf](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F255%2FWHW-1%2FMTH255-WHW-Week1_v1.pdf)\nDue: 2026-10-01\n\n## Instructions\n\nShow clear, organized work or a written explanation for every problem where required. Some problems are graded for correctness; an answer without supporting work does not earn full credit.\n\nYou may write on a printed copy, annotate the PDF digitally, or use your own paper with the same problem layout. Submit one seven-page PDF to **Gradescope → Written Homework 1**: the cover page followed by one page per problem, including blank problems. If a solution uses several sheets, combine them into one page for that problem. The source warns that submissions in the wrong format receive zero credit. See the linked PDF for the complete instructions.\n\nStudy answers, explanations, and reference graphics are authored additions to the linked source." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405301 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048960 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405301 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049512 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405301 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049517 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405301 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050033 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405303 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048299 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405303 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049494 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405303 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049576 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405303 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049878 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405303 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050728 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405304 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048191 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405307 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406099 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405307 1071 "Source question: 6.\n\nFind the curl of $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=\\langle6x,-2y,-7z\\rangle$. Enter the vector in angle-bracket form.\n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405307 1071 "Source question: 6.\n\nFind the curl of $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=\\langle6x,-2y,-7z\\rangle$. Enter the vector in angle-bracket form.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405307 1074 "Each component depends only on its own coordinate. Every cross derivative in the curl formula is zero. Return the zero vector, not a scalar." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405308 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406001 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405308 1071 "**Question 9 — Part B**\n\nChoose the graph of the spiral over the stated interval." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405308 1071 "**Question 9 — Part B**\n\nChoose the graph of the spiral over the stated interval.\n\n1. ![Choice 1](images/37/372d7deacc99d3b233815fe3d95c094d6cdaa98b5f3cd407d7e95a13667be2d7.png)\n\n2. ![Choice 2](images/76/7614756ffc7255c13fe186e66808f0e582f89f724f528ce6ee4a1a0c1366ea97.png)\n\n3. ![Choice 3](images/6c/6ced21be689964ce9e98983942308acc90bc892fc0e338872b5e1d8251b1c780.png)\n\n4. ![Choice 4](images/1b/1b13b961523e7938a69522c271d6baba8daed266bdb9a4fe316d02126a480445.png)" 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405308 1074 "In polar form, $r=t$ and $\\theta=t$. The spiral starts at the origin, moves counterclockwise, and ends at $(-5\\pi,0)$ after $2.5$ turns. At $t=9\\pi/2$ it crosses the positive $y$-axis near $14.14$, matching this graph." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405309 1064 "**6.1 Vector Fields**\n\nSource: [Macmillan Achieve](https://achieve.macmillanlearning.com/ihub/assessment/f188d950-dd73-11e0-9572-0800200c9a66/5a1db2a7-4295-4a4c-9dbb-ceb8b174a282/652f6185-14b0-408e-92fc-5cf7a73767b4?actualCourseId=5a1db2a7-4295-4a4c-9dbb-ceb8b174a282)\nDue: 2026-10-08" 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405309 1064 "**6.1 Vector Fields**\n\nSource: [Macmillan Achieve](https://achieve.macmillanlearning.com/ihub/assessment/f188d950-dd73-11e0-9572-0800200c9a66/5a1db2a7-4295-4a4c-9dbb-ceb8b174a282/652f6185-14b0-408e-92fc-5cf7a73767b4?actualCourseId=5a1db2a7-4295-4a4c-9dbb-ceb8b174a282)\nDue: 2026-10-08\nImported: 2026-10-01 · 12 questions\n\nStudy answers, explanations, and reference graphics are authored additions to the linked source." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405311 1068 #edb/ref 17592186405869 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405311 1071 "**Question 6 — Part C**\n\nAlternatively, compute the cross product below. What does the result tell you about $\\nabla\\varphi$ and the tangent plane?\n\n$\\langle\\varphi_z,0,-\\varphi_x\\rangle\\times\\langle0,\\varphi_z,-\\varphi_y\\rangle=$ ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405311 1071 "**Question 6 — Part C**\n\nAlternatively, compute the cross product below. What does the result tell you about $\\nabla\\varphi$ and the tangent plane?\n\n$\\langle\\varphi_z,0,-\\varphi_x\\rangle\\times\\langle0,\\varphi_z,-\\varphi_y\\rangle=$ {{field-1}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405311 1074 "The cross product is $\\langle\\varphi_x\\varphi_z,\\varphi_y\\varphi_z,\\varphi_z^2\\rangle=\\varphi_z\\nabla\\varphi$. A cross product of two independent tangent vectors is normal to their plane. For $\\varphi_z\\ne0$, it is a nonzero scalar multiple of the gradient, so the gradient is also normal. If $\\varphi_z=0$, these particular vectors do not establish the normal; a different local coordinate choice is needed at a regular point." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405312 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406287 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405312 1071 "Source question: 6.\n\nUse Stokes’ theorem to find $\\iint_S(\\nabla\\times\\mathbf F)\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,dS$, where $\\mathbf F=\\langle3z,8x,9y\\rangle$ and S is the paraboloid cap $z=6-x^2-y^2$, z≥3. **Source ambiguity:** No normal direction is given; use the upward normal for this study answer.\n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405312 1071 "Source question: 6.\n\nUse Stokes’ theorem to find $\\iint_S(\\nabla\\times\\mathbf F)\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,dS$, where $\\mathbf F=\\langle3z,8x,9y\\rangle$ and S is the paraboloid cap $z=6-x^2-y^2$, z≥3. **Source ambiguity:** No normal direction is given; use the upward normal for this study answer.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405312 1074 "The boundary is the circle x²+y²=3 at z=3. Replace the cap by the upward-oriented disk with the same boundary. The curl is (9,3,8), so its flux through the disk is 8 times its area, 8·3π=24π. A downward normal gives −24π. If “surface enclosed” were intended to include a closing base as well, the total curl flux of that closed surface would be 0." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405313 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048314 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405313 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050149 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405314 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406175 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405314 1071 "Source question: 5.\n\nUse the divergence theorem to find the outward flux of $\\mathbf F=\\langle2x-6y,9y-6z,7x-y\\rangle$ through the cube with faces x=±2, y=±2, z=±2.\n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405314 1071 "Source question: 5.\n\nUse the divergence theorem to find the outward flux of $\\mathbf F=\\langle2x-6y,9y-6z,7x-y\\rangle$ through the cube with faces x=±2, y=±2, z=±2.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405314 1074 "Divergence is 2+9+0=11. The cube has side length 4, not 2, and volume 64. Thus the flux is 11·64=704." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405315 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406190 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405315 1071 "**(b)** C consists of the line segment(s) joining $(8,\\pi/3)\\to(9,\\pi)$, in that order.\n\nIntegral: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405315 1071 "**(b)** C consists of the line segment(s) joining $(8,\\pi/3)\\to(9,\\pi)$, in that order.\n\nIntegral: {{integral}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405315 1074 "The potential is $f=e^x\\cos y$. All three paths have the same endpoints. Thus $f(9,\\pi)-f(8,\\pi/3)=-e^9-e^8/2$. Differentiating f verifies the two coefficients of the differential." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405317 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406107 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405317 1071 "Source question: 5.\n\n**Question 5**\n\nEliminate $t$ from $x(t)=8\\sin t$, $y(t)=5\\cos t$, $0\\le t\\le2\\pi$. Enter an exact rectangular equation: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405317 1071 "Source question: 5.\n\n**Question 5**\n\nEliminate $t$ from $x(t)=8\\sin t$, $y(t)=5\\cos t$, $0\\le t\\le2\\pi$. Enter an exact rectangular equation: {{field-1}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405317 1074 "Divide the coordinates by their amplitudes: $x/8=\\sin t$ and $y/5=\\cos t$. Squaring and adding gives $x^2/64+y^2/25=1$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405318 1074 "With $\\mathbf r=\\langle\\rho\\sin\\phi\\cos\\theta,\\rho\\sin\\phi\\sin\\theta,\\rho\\cos\\phi\\rangle$, the chain rule gives $w_q=2\\mathbf r\\cdot\\mathbf r_q$. Thus $w_\\rho=2\\rho(\\sin^2\\phi\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\phi\\sin^2\\theta+\\cos^2\\phi)=2\\rho$. The terms in $w_\\phi=2\\rho^2\\sin\\phi\\cos\\phi(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta-1)$ cancel, and the terms in $w_\\theta=2\\rho^2\\sin^2\\phi(-\\cos\\theta\\sin\\theta+\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta)$ cancel. Therefore $w_\\rho=2\\rho$, $w_\\phi=0$, and $w_\\theta=0$. This agrees with $w=\\rho^2$: temperature depends only on distance from the origin.\n\nCompute the coordinate derivatives before taking each dot product. At fixed radius, moving in either angular direction stays on the same sphere and cannot change $x^2+y^2+z^2$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405319 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049465 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405319 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050540 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405321 1068 #edb/ref 17592186405991 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405321 1071 "[Open in WeBWorK](https://webwork2.science.oregonstate.edu/webwork2/MTH_256_020_F2026/WebWork_1/2)\n\n**Question 2**\n\nSolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}-y=3e^t+36e^{5t}\n$$\n\nwith $y(0)=4$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405321 1071 "[Open in WeBWorK](https://webwork2.science.oregonstate.edu/webwork2/MTH_256_020_F2026/WebWork_1/2)\n\n**Question 2**\n\nSolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}-y=3e^t+36e^{5t}\n$$\n\nwith $y(0)=4$.\n\n$y(t)=$ {{field-1}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405321 1074 "Multiply by $e^{-t}$: $(e^{-t}y)\\prime=3+36e^{4t}$. Thus $e^{-t}y=3t+9e^{4t}+C$. At $0$, $4=9+C$, so $C=-5$. The resonant $3e^t$ forcing produces the $3te^t$ term." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405324 1089 #edb/ref 17592186051091 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405325 1068 #edb/ref 17592186405977 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405325 1071 "Source question: 1.\n\n**Question 1**\n\nWhich type of curve is described by $x(t)=9\\sin t$ and $y(t)=11\\cos t$?" 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405325 1071 "Source question: 1.\n\n**Question 1**\n\nWhich type of curve is described by $x(t)=9\\sin t$ and $y(t)=11\\cos t$?\n\n1. A hyperbola.\n\n2. A line.\n\n3. A circle.\n\n4. An ellipse.\n\n5. A parabola." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405325 1074 "Divide by the amplitudes and use $\\sin^2t+\\cos^2t=1$: $x^2/81+y^2/121=1$. Unequal positive semiaxes give an ellipse." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405327 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048239 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405327 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048424 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405327 1089 #edb/ref 17592186051090 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405329 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406238 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405329 1071 "Source question: 7.\n\nThe field $\\mathbf F=\\langle6x-5y+2,9-5x\\rangle$ is conservative. Find a potential f. Use K for the arbitrary constant and absorb into K as much as possible.\n\n$f(x,y)=$ ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405329 1071 "Source question: 7.\n\nThe field $\\mathbf F=\\langle6x-5y+2,9-5x\\rangle$ is conservative. Find a potential f. Use K for the arbitrary constant and absorb into K as much as possible.\n\n$f(x,y)=$ {{potential}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405329 1074 "Integrate $f_x=6x-5y+2$ to get $3x^2-5xy+2x+g(y)$. Matching $f_y=9-5x$ gives g′=9, hence g=9y+K. Differentiating both components verifies the result." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405333 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406016 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405333 1071 "Source question: 11.\n\nLet $\\mathbf F=\\langle x+y,3x-z,y\\rangle$ and let S be $z+2x+2y=4$ in the first octant. Find its outward flux $\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,dS$.\n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405333 1071 "Source question: 11.\n\nLet $\\mathbf F=\\langle x+y,3x-z,y\\rangle$ and let S be $z+2x+2y=4$ in the first octant. Find its outward flux $\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,dS$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405333 1074 "Outward from the first-octant tetrahedron gives vector area (2,2,1)dxdy on the plane. Substituting z=4−2x−2y yields F·(2,2,1)=12x+7y−8. On x≥0,y≥0,x+y≤2, area=2 and both first moments equal 4/3. Thus flux =12·4/3+7·4/3−16=28/3." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405335 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048299 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405335 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049494 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405335 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049576 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405335 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049878 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405335 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050728 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405336 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048546 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405342 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050295 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405342 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050866 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405345 1074 "All $k$ lines coincide: they describe the same geometric line. Every point on that line satisfies every equation, so there are infinitely many solutions.\n\nTwo distinct lines can share at most one point. Therefore infinitely many common points require every equation to describe the same line. Distinct parallel lines have no common points, so being parallel is not enough." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405346 1074 "Holding $y$ fixed gives $0=f_x(1)+f_y(0)+f_z z_x=f_x+f_z z_x$. Thus $z_x=-f_x/f_z$ if $f_z\\ne0$. Holding $x$ fixed similarly gives $0=f_x(0)+f_y(1)+f_z z_y$, hence $z_y=-f_y/f_z$.\n\nWhen differentiating with respect to $x$, the independent coordinate $y$ stays fixed but the dependent coordinate $z$ changes. Keep the $f_z z_x$ chain-rule term. The formulas require $f_z\\ne0$, the condition for solving locally for $z(x,y)$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405347 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049465 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405348 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406074 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405348 1071 "Source question: 10.\n\n**Question 10**\n\nFind the distance traveled along $x(t)=3\\cos t$, $y(t)=3\\sin t$ during $0\\le t\\le2\\pi$. Give an exact answer.\n\n$s=$ ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405348 1071 "Source question: 10.\n\n**Question 10**\n\nFind the distance traveled along $x(t)=3\\cos t$, $y(t)=3\\sin t$ during $0\\le t\\le2\\pi$. Give an exact answer.\n\n$s=$ {{field-1}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405348 1074 "The particle has speed $\\sqrt{(-3\\sin t)^2+(3\\cos t)^2}=3$. Integrating for $2\\pi$ units of parameter gives $s=6\\pi$. The displacement is zero, but the distance traveled is one circumference." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405350 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406124 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405350 1071 "**Question 2 — Part B**\n\n$y'(x)=\\sqrt{x},\\quad y(0)=4$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405350 1071 "**Question 2 — Part B**\n\n$y'(x)=\\sqrt{x},\\quad y(0)=4$.\n\n$y(x)=$ {{field-1}}, for $x\\ge0$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405350 1074 "Integrate $x^{1/2}$ to get $(2/3)x^{3/2}+C$. The initial condition sets $C=4$. This real solution is defined for $x\\ge0$; its derivative at the endpoint is understood from the right." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405358 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049465 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405361 1068 #edb/ref 17592186405928 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405361 1071 "**(c)** $\\mathbf F=\\langle-y,x\\rangle=-y\\mathbf i+x\\mathbf j$.\n\nPolar form: ____. Explain how the result agrees with the field plot." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405361 1071 "**(c)** $\\mathbf F=\\langle-y,x\\rangle=-y\\mathbf i+x\\mathbf j$.\n\nPolar form: {{polar}}. Explain how the result agrees with the field plot." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405361 1074 "**interpretation:** The radial coefficient $-y\\cos\\theta+x\\sin\\theta$ is 0; the angular coefficient $y\\sin\\theta+x\\cos\\theta$ is r. It circulates counterclockwise with length r.\n\nThe radial coefficient $-y\\cos\\theta+x\\sin\\theta$ is 0; the angular coefficient $y\\sin\\theta+x\\cos\\theta$ is r. It circulates counterclockwise with length r. Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$ and the basis identities from 4(b). Polar directions require r>0; in (a) and (c), the Cartesian field extends to the zero vector at the origin." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405362 1064 "**6.7 Stokes’ Theorem**\n\nDue: 2026-11-26\nSource: [Macmillan Achieve](https://achieve.macmillanlearning.com/ihub/assessment/f188d950-dd73-11e0-9572-0800200c9a66/5a1db2a7-4295-4a4c-9dbb-ceb8b174a282/d50b694c-c2bb-4457-ac38-dfafa8283ee5)" 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405362 1064 "**6.7 Stokes’ Theorem**\n\nDue: 2026-11-26\nSource: [Macmillan Achieve](https://achieve.macmillanlearning.com/ihub/assessment/f188d950-dd73-11e0-9572-0800200c9a66/5a1db2a7-4295-4a4c-9dbb-ceb8b174a282/d50b694c-c2bb-4457-ac38-dfafa8283ee5)\n\nStudy answers and explanations are authored additions, not publisher solutions.\n\n## Instructions\n\nUse exact symbolic notation, fractions, and component form or standard basis vectors as appropriate. Orientation assumptions are explicit where the source is incomplete. Source: Sullivan 2e Calculus, W.H. Freeman. Unlimited attempts, no incorrect-attempt penalty; up to 50 days late at a 2% daily penalty." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405366 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406113 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405366 1071 "Source question: 7.\n\nFind $\\iint_R5x\\,dA$, where R is enclosed by $5x-y=0$, $5x-y=8$, $x+y=0$, $x+y=3$. Use $u=5x-y$, $v=x+y$.\n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405366 1071 "Source question: 7.\n\nFind $\\iint_R5x\\,dA$, where R is enclosed by $5x-y=0$, $5x-y=8$, $x+y=0$, $x+y=3$. Use $u=5x-y$, $v=x+y$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405366 1074 "Invert: $x=(u+v)/6$, $y=(5v-u)/6$. The new rectangle is 0≤u≤8, 0≤v≤3, and $|J|=1/6$. Thus $I=\\frac5{36}\\int_0^8\\int_0^3(u+v)dvdu=\\frac5{36}(132)=55/3$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405368 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048575 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405368 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049152 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405369 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406024 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405369 1071 "**Question 3 — Part B**\n\nGive the domain in interval notation: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405369 1071 "**Question 3 — Part B**\n\nGive the domain in interval notation: {{field-1}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405369 1074 "The increasing function $x=t-6$ maps the included endpoints $t=0,2$ to $x=-6,-4$. Thus $-6\\le x\\le-4$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405370 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048923 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405370 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050418 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405373 1074 "Using the worksheet’s geometric meaning of integral curve, draw the entire right branch $x^2-y^2=1/4$, $x>0$, including its turn at $(1/2,0)$. One parametrization is $(x,y)=(\\tfrac12\\cosh s,\\tfrac12\\sinh s)$. Draw its visible portion within the supplied axes. Away from $y=0$, its slope is $dy/dx=\\cosh s/\\sinh s=x/y$. The whole curve is not a solution graph $y(x)$: for $x>1/2$ it has two $y$ values, and the explicit ODE is undefined at its vertical tangent $(1/2,0)$.\n\n![Authored reference graph](images/1a/1a426ad2bc023da1fc079d9ca7017c8735d1495114389a0d020cf1874195071d.png)\n\nHere “integral curve” means a geometric trajectory following the line directions, including a vertical turn; it is broader than a classical solution graph of $y\\prime=x/y$. The parametric tangent $(\\tfrac12\\sinh s,\\tfrac12\\cosh s)=(y,x)$ remains nonzero at the turn. Each upper/lower portion can separately define a solution for $x>1/2$, but their union cannot." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405375 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049768 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405375 1089 #edb/ref 17592186051091 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405376 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406086 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405376 1071 "Source question: 5.\n\nFind $\\partial(x,y,z)/\\partial(u,v,w)$ for $x=5u-14v$, $y=-6v+11w$, $z=15u-4w$.\n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405376 1071 "Source question: 5.\n\nFind $\\partial(x,y,z)/\\partial(u,v,w)$ for $x=5u-14v$, $y=-6v+11w$, $z=15u-4w$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405376 1074 "The matrix is $\\begin{pmatrix}5&-14&0\\\\0&-6&11\\\\15&0&-4\\end{pmatrix}$. Expansion along the first row gives $5(24)+14(0-165)=120-2310=-2190$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405378 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048960 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405378 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049859 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405379 1068 #edb/ref 17592186405954 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405379 1071 "Source question: 1.\n\nFind $\\partial(x,y)/\\partial(u,v)$ for $x=2u+15$, $y=-12+5v$.\n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405379 1071 "Source question: 1.\n\nFind $\\partial(x,y)/\\partial(u,v)$ for $x=2u+15$, $y=-12+5v$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405379 1074 "The derivative matrix is $\\begin{pmatrix}2&0\\\\0&5\\end{pmatrix}$, with determinant 10. Translation constants disappear under differentiation." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405382 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406188 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405382 1071 "Source question: 4.\n\nFind $\\nabla\\times\\mathbf F$ for $\\mathbf F=\\langle4\\sin(8xy),5\\cos(xy^8),8x\\rangle$.\n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405382 1071 "Source question: 4.\n\nFind $\\nabla\\times\\mathbf F$ for $\\mathbf F=\\langle4\\sin(8xy),5\\cos(xy^8),8x\\rangle$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405382 1074 "R_y−Q_z=0. P_z−R_x=−8. By the chain rule, Q_x=−5y⁸sin(xy⁸) and P_y=32xcos(8xy); subtract these to obtain the third component." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405384 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406134 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405384 1071 "Source question: 7.\n\n**Question 7**\n\nChoose the graph of $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=z\\mathbf k$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405384 1071 "Source question: 7.\n\n**Question 7**\n\nChoose the graph of $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=z\\mathbf k$.\n\n1. ![Choice 1](images/97/9724974f21977c050a42251a25487efa8273e9164369e2bb08b77aa537ed0ba6.png)\n\n2. ![Choice 2](images/6c/6c1abbaeb1eaf80867292350ed5a0750966cce2242c24d67e69ee2546f7ca050.png)\n\n3. ![Choice 3](images/c2/c2bb647a4098f3706ad9f90fcca52103d9c5b95d8e7c6b5d01a0de49cadf473b.png)\n\n4. ![Choice 4](images/1b/1b9471c36998e10bc10619591c03d729542d76c63538dcf2e0b52abc0ae1244e.png)" 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405384 1074 "For $z\\mathbf k$, arrows point upward at $z>0$ and downward at $z<0$, with magnitude $|z|$. The field vanishes on the plane $z=0$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405385 1089 #edb/ref 17592186051076 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405388 1074 "Using the cylindrical volume element $dV=r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta$,\n\n$$V=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\int_a^b\\int_{g(r)}^{f(r)}r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta=2\\pi\\int_a^b r[f(r)-g(r)]\\,dr=2\\pi\\int_a^b[xf(x)-xg(x)]\\,dx.$$\n\nThe last step only renames the dummy variable. As usual, the functions must define an integrable solid of finite volume for this finite-volume formula.\n\nThe factor $r$ is the Jacobian. Integrating over height gives $r(f-g)$, and integrating over the full angle gives $2\\pi$. The assumptions $r\\ge0$ and $f\\ge g$ make the shell contribution nonnegative." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405389 1068 #edb/ref 17592186405913 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405389 1071 "Source question: 2.\n\nUse the **Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals** to evaluate $\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot d\\mathbf r$. The field $\\mathbf F=\\langle13y^2+26x,26xy\\rangle$ is conservative with the supplied potential $f=13xy^2+13x^2$. The piecewise smooth curve joins $(2,2)$ to $(-3,8)$.\n\nIntegral: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405389 1071 "Source question: 2.\n\nUse the **Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals** to evaluate $\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot d\\mathbf r$. The field $\\mathbf F=\\langle13y^2+26x,26xy\\rangle$ is conservative with the supplied potential $f=13xy^2+13x^2$. The piecewise smooth curve joins $(2,2)$ to $(-3,8)$.\n\nIntegral: {{integral}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405389 1074 "$f(-3,8)=-2496+117=-2379$ and $f(2,2)=104+52=156$. The difference is −2535." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405391 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049465 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405396 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406311 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405396 1071 "Source question: 1.\n\n**Question 1**\n\nLet $P(x,y,z)$, $Q(x,y,z)$, and $R(x,y,z)$ be scalar functions on a region $E$. True or false: the formula $F(x,y,z)=P(x,y,z)+Q(x,y,z)+R(x,y,z)$ defines a vector field on $E$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405396 1071 "Source question: 1.\n\n**Question 1**\n\nLet $P(x,y,z)$, $Q(x,y,z)$, and $R(x,y,z)$ be scalar functions on a region $E$. True or false: the formula $F(x,y,z)=P(x,y,z)+Q(x,y,z)+R(x,y,z)$ defines a vector field on $E$.\n\n1. False\n\n2. True" 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405396 1074 "A vector field assigns a vector to each point. The displayed sum is scalar; a three-dimensional vector field has the form $\\mathbf F=P\\mathbf i+Q\\mathbf j+R\\mathbf k$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405397 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048832 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405397 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050196 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405398 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048960 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405398 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049859 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405399 1068 #edb/ref 17592186405864 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405399 1071 "**Question 2 — Part A**\n\nFind\n\n$$\n\\frac{d\\mathbf r}{dt}=\\mathbf r'(t)=\\langle x'(t),y'(t),z'(t)\\rangle.\n$$" 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405399 1071 "**Question 2 — Part A**\n\nFind\n\n$$\n\\frac{d\\mathbf r}{dt}=\\mathbf r'(t)=\\langle x'(t),y'(t),z'(t)\\rangle.\n$$\n\n$\\mathbf r\\,\\prime(t)=$ {{field-1}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405399 1074 "Differentiate each component. In particular, $(\\ln\\sec t)\\prime=\\sec t\\tan t/\\sec t=\\tan t$ wherever the real-valued logarithm is defined." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405400 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406185 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405400 1071 "Source question: 12.\n\n**Question 12**\n\nA small mass $m$ (kg) produces gravitational potential $u=-Gm/r$, where $r$ is the distance from the mass in meters, $G=6.67\\times10^{-11}\\ \\mathrm{N\\,m^2/kg^2}$, and $\\mathbf r=x\\mathbf i+y\\mathbf j+z\\mathbf k$. If $\\mathbf g=-\\nabla u$, choose the expression for $\\mathbf g$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405400 1071 "Source question: 12.\n\n**Question 12**\n\nA small mass $m$ (kg) produces gravitational potential $u=-Gm/r$, where $r$ is the distance from the mass in meters, $G=6.67\\times10^{-11}\\ \\mathrm{N\\,m^2/kg^2}$, and $\\mathbf r=x\\mathbf i+y\\mathbf j+z\\mathbf k$. If $\\mathbf g=-\\nabla u$, choose the expression for $\\mathbf g$.\n\n1. $\\mathbf g=\\dfrac{Gm}{r^3}\\mathbf r$\n\n2. $\\mathbf g=-\\dfrac{Gm}{r^3}\\mathbf r$\n\n3. $\\mathbf g=-\\dfrac{Gm}{r^2}$\n\n4. $\\mathbf g=-\\dfrac12\\dfrac{Gm}{r^3}\\mathbf r$" 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405400 1074 "Using $r=\\sqrt{x^2+y^2+z^2}$ gives $\\nabla(1/r)=-\\mathbf r/r^3$. Hence $\\nabla u=Gm\\mathbf r/r^3$ and $\\mathbf g=-Gm\\mathbf r/r^3$, directed toward the mass." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405402 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406294 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405402 1071 "**(b)** Find the unique two-dimensional field that is undefined on y=x, unit everywhere else, and points away from and orthogonal to y=x.\n\n$\\mathbf F(x,y)=$ ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405402 1071 "**(b)** Find the unique two-dimensional field that is undefined on y=x, unit everywhere else, and points away from and orthogonal to y=x.\n\n$\\mathbf F(x,y)=$ {{field}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405402 1074 "The closest point on the line is $((x+y)/2,(x+y)/2)$. The displacement away from that point is proportional to $(x-y,y-x)$, perpendicular to (1,1). Its length is $\\sqrt2|x-y|$. Normalize; the sign automatically reverses across the line." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405404 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406193 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405404 1071 "Source exercise: **1.2.1**, [printed page 42 (PDF page 54)](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F341%2FKuttler-LinearAlgebra-AFirstCourse-2017A.pdf#page=54).\n\n**Question 4**\n\nFind the point $(x_1,y_1)$ which lies on both lines,\n\n$$\nx+3y=1 \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad 4x-y=3.\n$$\n\nEnter the exact ordered pair $(x_1,y_1)$, with fractions simplified: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405404 1071 "Source exercise: **1.2.1**, [printed page 42 (PDF page 54)](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F341%2FKuttler-LinearAlgebra-AFirstCourse-2017A.pdf#page=54).\n\n**Question 4**\n\nFind the point $(x_1,y_1)$ which lies on both lines,\n\n$$\nx+3y=1 \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad 4x-y=3.\n$$\n\nEnter the exact ordered pair $(x_1,y_1)$, with fractions simplified: {{field-1}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405404 1074 "A common point satisfies both equations. Multiply $x+3y=1$ by $4$ to get $4x+12y=4$, then subtract $4x-y=3$. This gives $13y=1$, so $y=1/13$ and $x=1-3/13=10/13$. Thus the point is $\\left(\\frac{10}{13},\\frac{1}{13}\\right)$. Substitution gives $1$ and $3$ in the two original equations. Enter $x$ first and $y$ second." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405405 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406216 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405405 1071 "Source question: 1.\n\nFind $\\int_C21x^2y\\,ds$ for $x=\\cos(13t),\\ y=\\cos(13t),\\ 0\\le t\\le\\pi/26$.\n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405405 1071 "Source question: 1.\n\nFind $\\int_C21x^2y\\,ds$ for $x=\\cos(13t),\\ y=\\cos(13t),\\ 0\\le t\\le\\pi/26$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405405 1074 "$ds=13\\sqrt2\\sin(13t)dt$ on this interval. With u=cos(13t), the integral is $21\\sqrt2\\int_0^1u^3du=21\\sqrt2/4$. The straight segment from (1,1) to (0,0) gives the same result." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405406 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406141 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405406 1071 "Source question: 5.\n\nFind the curl of $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=\\langle9xyz,2xz,5z\\rangle$. \n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405406 1071 "Source question: 5.\n\nFind the curl of $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=\\langle9xyz,2xz,5z\\rangle$. \n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405406 1074 "$R_y-Q_z=0-2x$, $P_z-R_x=9xy-0$, and $Q_x-P_y=2z-9xz$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405408 1068 #edb/ref 17592186405843 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405408 1071 "Source question: 5.\n\nFind $\\int_C10x\\,ds$ along $x=3y^3$ from (0,0) to (3,1).\n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405408 1071 "Source question: 5.\n\nFind $\\int_C10x\\,ds$ along $x=3y^3$ from (0,0) to (3,1).\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405408 1074 "$ds=\\sqrt{1+81y^4}dy$. Integrate $30y^3\\sqrt{1+81y^4}$ from 0 to 1; u=1+81y⁴ yields $\\frac5{81}[u^{3/2}]_1^{82}$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405410 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406257 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405410 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406314 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405410 1071 "Source question: 7.\n\nFor $f(x,y)=2xe^y$, find its line integrals with respect to x and y along $5x-y=8$ from (0,−8) to (1,−3).\n\n$\\int_C f\\,dx=$ ____.\n\n$\\int_C f\\,dy=$ ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405410 1071 "Source question: 7.\n\nFor $f(x,y)=2xe^y$, find its line integrals with respect to x and y along $5x-y=8$ from (0,−8) to (1,−3).\n\n$\\int_C f\\,dx=$ {{dx}}.\n\n$\\int_C f\\,dy=$ {{dy}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405410 1074 "Use y=5x−8 and dy=5dx, x from 0 to 1. Integration by parts gives $\\int_0^1 2xe^{5x-8}dx=[(2/25)e^{5x-8}(5x-1)]_0^1$. The dy integral is five times the dx integral." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405411 1074 "Area is base times perpendicular height: $A=\\lVert\\mathbf u\\rVert\\,\\lVert\\mathbf v\\rVert\\sin\\theta$. By the magnitude formula for a cross product, this equals $\\lVert\\mathbf u\\times\\mathbf v\\rVert$.\n\nUse $\\lVert\\mathbf u\\rVert$ as the base length and the height from Part A. The cross product is a vector; its magnitude is the nonnegative scalar area. Parallel vectors give zero area." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405414 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406022 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405414 1071 "Source question: 5.\n\nFind $\\nabla\\times\\mathbf F$ for $\\mathbf F=\\langle3x+y,3y+9z,5z+6x\\rangle$.\n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405414 1071 "Source question: 5.\n\nFind $\\nabla\\times\\mathbf F$ for $\\mathbf F=\\langle3x+y,3y+9z,5z+6x\\rangle$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405414 1074 "The three components are R_y−Q_z=0−9, P_z−R_x=0−6, and Q_x−P_y=0−1." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405416 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049512 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405416 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050037 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405416 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050164 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405417 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406092 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405417 1071 "Source question: 4.\n\nUse the **Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals** to evaluate $\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot d\\mathbf r$. The field $\\mathbf F=\\left\\langle\\frac{5x}{5x^2+3y^2},\\frac{3y}{5x^2+3y^2}\\right\\rangle$ is conservative with the supplied potential $f=\\ln\\sqrt{5x^2+3y^2}$. The piecewise smooth curve joins $(3,4)$ to $(5,12)$.\n\nIntegral: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405417 1071 "Source question: 4.\n\nUse the **Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals** to evaluate $\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot d\\mathbf r$. The field $\\mathbf F=\\left\\langle\\frac{5x}{5x^2+3y^2},\\frac{3y}{5x^2+3y^2}\\right\\rangle$ is conservative with the supplied potential $f=\\ln\\sqrt{5x^2+3y^2}$. The piecewise smooth curve joins $(3,4)$ to $(5,12)$.\n\nIntegral: {{integral}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405417 1074 "$5(5)^2+3(12)^2=557$ and $5(3)^2+3(4)^2=93$. Thus $f(B)-f(A)=\\ln\\sqrt{557}-\\ln\\sqrt{93}$. The curve is understood to lie in the field domain, excluding the origin." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405418 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048960 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405418 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049859 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405419 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406112 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405419 1071 "Source question: 2.\n\nFind $\\partial(x,y)/\\partial(u,v)$ for $x=5u+4v$, $y=6u-v/7$.\n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405419 1071 "Source question: 2.\n\nFind $\\partial(x,y)/\\partial(u,v)$ for $x=5u+4v$, $y=6u-v/7$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405419 1074 "The determinant is $5(-1/7)-4(6)=-5/7-24=-173/7$. A Jacobian determinant retains its sign." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405420 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406312 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405420 1071 "**Question 4 — Part C**\n\nSolve the initial value problem.\n\n\n\nNote: You can earn partial credit on this problem." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405420 1071 "**Question 4 — Part C**\n\nSolve the initial value problem.\n\n\n\nNote: You can earn partial credit on this problem.\n\n$y(x)=$ {{field-1}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405420 1074 "Insert $x=1$, $y=-4$ into $(3/4)x+Cx^9$: $C=-4-3/4=-19/4$. Substitution in $xy\\prime=9y-6x$ checks both sides as $(3/4)x-(171/4)x^9$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405421 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048314 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405421 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050149 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405422 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406037 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405422 1071 "**Question 4 — Part A**\n\nIdentify an integrating factor, $\\alpha(x)$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405422 1071 "**Question 4 — Part A**\n\nIdentify an integrating factor, $\\alpha(x)$.\n\n$\\alpha(x)=$ {{field-1}}, on $x>0$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405422 1074 "Normalize first: $y\\prime-(9/x)y=-6$. Then $\\alpha=e^{\\int-9/x\\,dx}=x^{-9}$ on the positive interval containing $x=1$. Any nonzero constant multiple is also an integrating factor." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405423 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406313 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405423 1071 "Source question: 2.\n\n**Question 2**\n\nTrue or false: the gradient of a differentiable scalar function $f$ is a vector field." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405423 1071 "Source question: 2.\n\n**Question 2**\n\nTrue or false: the gradient of a differentiable scalar function $f$ is a vector field.\n\n1. True\n\n2. False" 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405423 1074 "The gradient assigns the vector of partial derivatives to each point: $\\nabla f=\\langle f_x,f_y,f_z\\rangle$ in three dimensions. This is a vector field wherever those derivatives exist." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405425 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048495 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405427 1089 #edb/ref 17592186051006 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405429 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050149 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405429 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050875 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405431 1064 "Date: 2026-09-29\nDue: 2026-09-29\n\nSource: [Original worksheet](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F256%2FR1%2F256-R1.pdf)" 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405431 1064 "Date: 2026-09-29\nDue: 2026-09-29\n\nSource: [Original worksheet](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F256%2FR1%2F256-R1.pdf)\n\nThe purpose of this recitation is to review material from previous calculus classes that will be important during the quarter.\n\nStudy answers, explanations, and reference graphics are authored additions to the linked source." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405432 1089 #edb/ref 17592186051091 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405439 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406083 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405439 1071 "Source question: 22.\n\nAn object follows $\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle16t,14t-3t^2\\rangle$ from t=0 to t=7. A force has magnitude proportional to speed and direction opposite velocity. Find its work W, using k for the proportionality constant.\n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405439 1071 "Source question: 22.\n\nAn object follows $\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle16t,14t-3t^2\\rangle$ from t=0 to t=7. A force has magnitude proportional to speed and direction opposite velocity. Find its work W, using k for the proportionality constant.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405439 1074 "The force is $F=-k r\\prime$, so $W=-k\\int_0^7\\|r\\prime\\|^2dt=-k\\int_0^7[256+(14-6t)^2]dt=-k(1792+1372)$. The negative sign agrees with resistance to motion." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405440 1068 #edb/ref 17592186405997 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405440 1071 "Source question: 14.\n\nFind $\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot d\\mathbf r$ for $\\mathbf F=\\langle x^2y,xy^2\\rangle$, $\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle2t,t^2\\rangle$, $1\\le t\\le2$.\n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405440 1071 "Source question: 14.\n\nFind $\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot d\\mathbf r$ for $\\mathbf F=\\langle x^2y,xy^2\\rangle$, $\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle2t,t^2\\rangle$, $1\\le t\\le2$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405440 1074 "$F(r)=(4t^4,2t^5)$ and $r\\prime=(2,2t)$, so integrate $8t^4+4t^6$: $[8t^5/5+4t^7/7]_1^2=248/5+508/7$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405441 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048923 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405441 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050418 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405443 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048314 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405443 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050149 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405444 1074 "$\\frac{d}{dx}(e^xy)=e^xy+e^xy\\prime$. By the given differential equation this derivative equals $0$.\n\nThe product rule combines the two terms of the ODE into one derivative. The zero is the derivative of the product, not a claim that $y$ or $e^xy$ itself must be zero." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405445 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049366 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405446 1064 "**Math 255 — Recitation Worksheet 2**\n\nDue: unknown\nSource: [Original worksheet](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F255%2FR-2%2F255-R2.pdf)" 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405446 1064 "**Math 255 — Recitation Worksheet 2**\n\nDue: unknown\nSource: [Original worksheet](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F255%2FR-2%2F255-R2.pdf)\n\nStudy answers and explanations are authored additions, not publisher solutions.\n\n## Instructions\n\nGraded for completeness. Work on the printed worksheet, write/type on the PDF, or use your own paper with the same layout. Provide first name, last name, and OSU student ID on the cover. Submit one PDF to Gradescope under **Recitation Worksheet 2**, preserving the six-page template: cover followed by one page per problem, including blank problems. Scan printed work; digital work already has the required format. For separate paper, photograph all pages and merge them; combine multiple pages for one problem into a single page (a collage). Incorrectly formatted submissions receive 0. No due date is stated in the PDF." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405450 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406018 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405450 1071 "**Question 3 — Part D**\n\nUse the above to determine what $y(x)$ must be equal to." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405450 1071 "**Question 3 — Part D**\n\nUse the above to determine what $y(x)$ must be equal to.\n\n$y(x)=$ {{field-1}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405450 1074 "From $e^xy=y_0$, divide by $e^x>0$ to get $y=y_0e^{-x}$. Its derivative is $-y_0e^{-x}=-y$, and at $x=0$ its value is $y_0$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405452 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406327 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405452 1071 "Source question: 6.\n\nUse the divergence theorem to find the outward flux of $\\mathbf F=\\langle5x,3y^2,9z^2\\rangle$ through the entire boundary of the solid cylinder $x^2+y^2\\le9$, 0≤z≤5.\n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405452 1071 "Source question: 6.\n\nUse the divergence theorem to find the outward flux of $\\mathbf F=\\langle5x,3y^2,9z^2\\rangle$ through the entire boundary of the solid cylinder $x^2+y^2\\le9$, 0≤z≤5.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405452 1074 "Divergence is 5+6y+18z. The y term integrates to zero over each centered disk. The area is 9π, so the integral is $9\\pi\\int_0^5(5+18z)dz=9\\pi(25+225)=2250\\pi$. The boundary includes both end disks." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405454 1089 #edb/ref 17592186051091 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405456 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406171 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405456 1071 "Source question: 10.\n\nDetermine whether the following force is a conservative vector field.\n\n$\\mathbf F=\\langle10xy,yz,8xz\\rangle$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405456 1071 "Source question: 10.\n\nDetermine whether the following force is a conservative vector field.\n\n$\\mathbf F=\\langle10xy,yz,8xz\\rangle$.\n\n1. The field is conservative.\n\n2. The field is not conservative." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405456 1074 "Curl F=(−y,−8z,−10x), which is not identically zero. Therefore F cannot be a gradient field on all of space. For instance, P_y=10x differs from Q_x=0." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405459 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406049 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405459 1071 "Source question: 9.\n\nThe folium of Descartes is parameterized by $x(t)=8t/(1+t^3)$, $y(t)=8t^2/(1+t^3)$. Use **Green’s theorem** to find the area enclosed by its loop.\n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405459 1071 "Source question: 9.\n\nThe folium of Descartes is parameterized by $x(t)=8t/(1+t^3)$, $y(t)=8t^2/(1+t^3)$. Use **Green’s theorem** to find the area enclosed by its loop.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405459 1074 "The counterclockwise loop runs from t=0 to t→∞. Green’s area formula gives $A=\\frac12\\int_0^\\infty(xy′-yx′)dt=32\\int_0^\\infty t^2/(1+t^3)^2dt$. With u=1+t³ this is $\\frac{32}{3}\\int_1^\\infty u^{-2}du=32/3$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405462 1068 #edb/ref 17592186405877 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405462 1071 "Source question: 3.\n\nUse the **Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals** to evaluate $\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot d\\mathbf r$. The field $\\mathbf F=\\langle14xe^{7x^2+2y^2},4ye^{7x^2+2y^2}\\rangle$ is conservative with the supplied potential $f=e^{7x^2+2y^2}$. The piecewise smooth curve joins $(0,0)$ to $(1,1)$.\n\nIntegral: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405462 1071 "Source question: 3.\n\nUse the **Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals** to evaluate $\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot d\\mathbf r$. The field $\\mathbf F=\\langle14xe^{7x^2+2y^2},4ye^{7x^2+2y^2}\\rangle$ is conservative with the supplied potential $f=e^{7x^2+2y^2}$. The piecewise smooth curve joins $(0,0)$ to $(1,1)$.\n\nIntegral: {{integral}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405462 1074 "The final exponent is 7+2=9 and the initial exponent is 0. Thus f(B)−f(A)=e⁹−1." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405464 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048314 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405464 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050875 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405469 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050935 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405469 1089 #edb/ref 17592186051006 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405471 1064 "**6.2 Line Integrals**\n\nDue: 2026-10-15\nSource: [Macmillan Achieve](https://achieve.macmillanlearning.com/ihub/assessment/f188d950-dd73-11e0-9572-0800200c9a66/5a1db2a7-4295-4a4c-9dbb-ceb8b174a282/cad9d5c1-5041-449d-b720-8f2e67d796cc)" 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405471 1064 "**6.2 Line Integrals**\n\nDue: 2026-10-15\nSource: [Macmillan Achieve](https://achieve.macmillanlearning.com/ihub/assessment/f188d950-dd73-11e0-9572-0800200c9a66/5a1db2a7-4295-4a4c-9dbb-ceb8b174a282/cad9d5c1-5041-449d-b720-8f2e67d796cc)\n\nStudy answers and explanations are authored additions, not publisher solutions.\n\n## Instructions\n\nGive exact answers with symbolic notation and fractions as needed. Source: Sullivan 2e Calculus, W.H. Freeman. All 24 assigned questions are retained; questions 7 and 8 each have separate dx and dy answer slots. The live platform allows unlimited attempts without an incorrect-attempt penalty and submissions up to 50 days late at a 2% daily penalty." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405473 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406069 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405473 1071 "Source question: 4.\n\nUse the divergence theorem to find the outward flux of $\\mathbf F=\\langle5xy+2z,9y^2+8,-8x-9y\\rangle$ through the surface of the solid bounded by x+y+z=2 and the three coordinate planes.\n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405473 1071 "Source question: 4.\n\nUse the divergence theorem to find the outward flux of $\\mathbf F=\\langle5xy+2z,9y^2+8,-8x-9y\\rangle$ through the surface of the solid bounded by x+y+z=2 and the three coordinate planes.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405473 1074 "Divergence is 5y+18y=23y. The first-octant tetrahedron has volume 2³/6=4/3 and centroid y-coordinate 2/4=1/2. Hence ∭23y dV=23·(1/2)·(4/3)=46/3. Equivalently integrate 23y with 0≤x≤2, 0≤y≤2−x, 0≤z≤2−x−y." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405475 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406081 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405475 1071 "Source question: 4.\n\nFind $\\iint_Sx^2z\\,dS$ on the cylindrical surface $x^2+y^2=64$, $0\\le z\\le5$.\n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405475 1071 "Source question: 4.\n\nFind $\\iint_Sx^2z\\,dS$ on the cylindrical surface $x^2+y^2=64$, $0\\le z\\le5$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405475 1074 "Parameterize (8cosθ,8sinθ,z); the surface element is 8dθ dz. Thus $I=512\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\cos^2\\theta d\\theta\\int_0^5z\\,dz=512\\pi(25/2)=6400\\pi$. S contains only the side, not end disks." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405476 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048314 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405476 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050875 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405479 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050168 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405479 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050537 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405484 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406305 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405484 1071 "**Question 3 — Part B**\n\nUse Part A to find $dy/dx$ for the circle $x^2+y^2=1$.\n\n$\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=$ ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405484 1071 "**Question 3 — Part B**\n\nUse Part A to find $dy/dx$ for the circle $x^2+y^2=1$.\n\n$\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=$ {{field-1}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405484 1074 "With $f=x^2+y^2$, $f_x=2x$ and $f_y=2y$. Thus $dy/dx=-2x/(2y)=-x/y$ where $y\\ne0$. Direct differentiation gives the same equation, $2x+2y\\,dy/dx=0$. At $(\\pm1,0)$ the tangent is vertical." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405486 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050149 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405486 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050875 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405488 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050295 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405488 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050866 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405494 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406161 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405494 1071 "**Question 4 — Part A**\n\nEnter the rectangular equation: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405494 1071 "**Question 4 — Part A**\n\nEnter the rectangular equation: {{field-1}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405494 1074 "Squaring and adding gives $x^2+y^2=25(\\sin^2t+\\cos^2t)=25$. The parameter interval traces the whole circle." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405497 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049465 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405497 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050540 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405501 1074 "Assume $\\varphi_z\\ne0$ and parametrize the surface locally by $\\mathbf r(x,y)=\\langle x,y,z(x,y)\\rangle$. Its coordinate tangent vectors are $\\mathbf r_x=\\langle1,0,-\\varphi_x/\\varphi_z\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf r_y=\\langle0,1,-\\varphi_y/\\varphi_z\\rangle$. Multiplying each by $\\varphi_z$ gives the two stated tangent vectors.\n\nThe first tangent follows a curve with $y$ fixed, and the second follows one with $x$ fixed. When $\\varphi_z\\ne0$, they are independent and span the tangent plane. If that partial derivative vanishes at a regular point, choose another coordinate with nonzero partial derivative for the local parametrization." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405509 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406277 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405509 1071 "**Question 2 — Part A**\n\n$y'(x)=x^4,\\quad y(0)=5$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405509 1071 "**Question 2 — Part A**\n\n$y'(x)=x^4,\\quad y(0)=5$.\n\n$y(x)=$ {{field-1}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405509 1074 "Integrating $x^4$ gives $x^5/5+C$. The value at $0$ fixes $C=5$. Differentiating the result recovers $x^4$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405510 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048684 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405510 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050796 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405511 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406231 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405511 1071 "Source question: 12.\n\nFind $\\iint_S(x+4y)dS$ for $\\mathbf r(u,v)=\\langle6u\\cos v,u^2+1,4u\\cos v\\rangle$, $0\\le u\\le1$, $0\\le v\\le\\pi/2$.\n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405511 1071 "Source question: 12.\n\nFind $\\iint_S(x+4y)dS$ for $\\mathbf r(u,v)=\\langle6u\\cos v,u^2+1,4u\\cos v\\rangle$, $0\\le u\\le1$, $0\\le v\\le\\pi/2$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405511 1074 "$r_u=(6\\cos v,2u,4\\cos v)$ and $r_v=(-6u\\sin v,0,-4u\\sin v)$. Their cross product is $(-8u^2\\sin v,0,12u^2\\sin v)$, with norm $4\\sqrt{13}u^2\\sin v$. The integrand is $6u\\cos v+4u^2+4$. Integrate in v, then u: $4\\sqrt{13}\\int_0^1u^2(3u+4u^2+4)du=4\\sqrt{13}(3/4+4/5+4/3)$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405514 1089 #edb/ref 17592186051076 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405515 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048684 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405515 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050796 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405517 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406034 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405517 1071 "Source question: 7.\n\nUse the divergence theorem to find the outward flux of $\\mathbf F=\\langle5yz,3xz,-9xy\\rangle$ through the tetrahedron with vertices (0,0,0), (5,0,0), (0,5,0), and (0,0,5).\n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405517 1071 "Source question: 7.\n\nUse the divergence theorem to find the outward flux of $\\mathbf F=\\langle5yz,3xz,-9xy\\rangle$ through the tetrahedron with vertices (0,0,0), (5,0,0), (0,5,0), and (0,0,5).\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405517 1074 "P is independent of x, Q of y, and R of z, so divergence is 0 everywhere. Its integral over the tetrahedron is therefore 0. The field is smooth on the entire solid." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405518 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049465 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405521 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406028 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405521 1071 "Source question: 10.\n\nUse **Green’s theorem** to find the work of $\\mathbf F=5x\\mathbf i+2y^2\\mathbf j$ along $x=t,y=t^2$, $0\\le t\\le3$, then back to (0,0) along $x=t,y=3t$.\n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405521 1071 "Source question: 10.\n\nUse **Green’s theorem** to find the work of $\\mathbf F=5x\\mathbf i+2y^2\\mathbf j$ along $x=t,y=t^2$, $0\\le t\\le3$, then back to (0,0) along $x=t,y=3t$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405521 1074 "The return line is traversed with t decreasing from 3 to 0. Green’s theorem gives $\\iint_R(Q_x-P_y)dA=0$, since both cross partials vanish. A check is the global potential $5x^2/2+2y^3/3$: its net change around the closed path is zero." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405523 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048923 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405523 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048960 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405523 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049366 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405523 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050033 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405525 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406026 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405525 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406316 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405525 1071 "**Question 1 — Part C**\n\nIn which direction does the voltage increase/decrease most rapidly at $P$?" 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405525 1071 "**Question 1 — Part C**\n\nIn which direction does the voltage increase/decrease most rapidly at $P$?\n\nUnit direction of fastest increase: {{field-1}}.\n\nUnit direction of fastest decrease: {{field-2}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405525 1074 "Normalize $\\langle12,0,16\\rangle$ by its magnitude $20$ for increase. Negate it for decrease. Their directional derivatives are respectively $20$ and $-20$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405526 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406096 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405526 1071 "**(a)** Given $\\nabla f=3x\\mathbf i+8y\\mathbf j$, find f. Use K for the arbitrary constant and absorb into K as much as possible.\n\n$f(x,y)=$ ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405526 1071 "**(a)** Given $\\nabla f=3x\\mathbf i+8y\\mathbf j$, find f. Use K for the arbitrary constant and absorb into K as much as possible.\n\n$f(x,y)=$ {{potential}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405526 1074 "Integrate 3x in x, then match the y derivative to 8y. The two partial derivatives of $3x^2/2+4y^2+K$ are exactly 3x and 8y." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405528 1074 "**proof:** Expand the first right-hand side: $(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)\\mathbf i+(\\cos\\theta\\sin\\theta-\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta)\\mathbf j=\\mathbf i$. Expand the second: $(\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta-\\cos\\theta\\sin\\theta)\\mathbf i+(\\sin^2\\theta+\\cos^2\\theta)\\mathbf j=\\mathbf j$.\n\nSubstitute the given basis vectors and collect Cartesian components. Mixed terms cancel and squared sine/cosine terms sum to 1." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405529 1068 #edb/ref 17592186405863 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405529 1071 "Source question: 10.\n\nFind $\\iint_R10x\\,dA$ over the ellipse $x^2/16+y^2/49\\le1$.\n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405529 1071 "Source question: 10.\n\nFind $\\iint_R10x\\,dA$ over the ellipse $x^2/16+y^2/49\\le1$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405529 1074 "Use x=4u,y=7v, so the region is the unit disk and dA=28du dv. The transformed integrand is 1120u, odd across u=0; symmetric halves cancel." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405530 1074 "Expanding along the first row gives the vector $\\langle u_2v_3-u_3v_2,\\ u_3v_1-u_1v_3,\\ u_1v_2-u_2v_1\\rangle=\\mathbf u\\times\\mathbf v$. Its magnitude is the area by Part B. Equivalently, $A=\\sqrt{(u_2v_3-u_3v_2)^2+(u_3v_1-u_1v_3)^2+(u_1v_2-u_2v_1)^2}$.\n\nThe determinant with basis vectors in its first row is cross-product notation, so its expansion is a vector. Keep the minus sign in the middle cofactor and take the vector magnitude to obtain area." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405531 1074 "Yes. All three lines meet at $\\left(\\frac35,\\frac15\\right)$.\n\nFrom $x+2y=1$, write $x=1-2y$. Substitute into $2x-y=1$:\n\n$$2(1-2y)-y=1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad 2-5y=1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y=\\frac15.$$\n\nThen $x=1-2/5=3/5$. Check the remaining equation:\n\n$$4\\left(\\frac35\\right)+3\\left(\\frac15\\right)=\\frac{12}{5}+\\frac35=3.$$\n\nThe point obtained from the first two lines lies on the third, so it is common to all three.\n\nSolving two equations gives a candidate, not yet a solution to a three-equation system. Always substitute the candidate into the remaining equation. Here it satisfies the third equation as well. The first two lines are distinct and have only one intersection, so the verified common point is unique." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405532 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406139 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405532 1071 "Let S be the surface of a solid E satisfying the assumptions of the divergence theorem. With outward normal n, choose the correct statement." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405532 1071 "Let S be the surface of a solid E satisfying the assumptions of the divergence theorem. With outward normal n, choose the correct statement.\n\n1. $\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,dS=\\iint_S\\mathbf G\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,dS$\n\n2. The relation depends on the surface S.\n\n3. $\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,dS<\\iint_S\\mathbf G\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,dS$\n\n4. $\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,dS>\\iint_S\\mathbf G\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,dS$" 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405532 1074 "The divergence theorem gives each outward flux as 30·Volume(E), so they are equal for every eligible solid. Different component functions need not produce different total fluxes when their divergences agree." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405533 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406056 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405533 1071 "Source question: 6.\n\nFind $\\int_C(2x+7y)\\,ds$ for $x=\\sin(5t),\\ y=3\\sin(5t),\\ 0\\le t\\le\\pi/5$.\n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405533 1071 "Source question: 6.\n\nFind $\\int_C(2x+7y)\\,ds$ for $x=\\sin(5t),\\ y=3\\sin(5t),\\ 0\\le t\\le\\pi/5$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405533 1074 "The speed is $5\\sqrt{10}|\\cos(5t)|$ and the integrand is 23sin(5t). With u=5t, $I=23\\sqrt{10}\\int_0^\\pi\\sin u|\\cos u|du=23\\sqrt{10}$. Split at π/2: each half contributes 1/2. The path retraces the segment, so both passes count." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405535 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050514 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405537 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048239 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405538 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048575 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405538 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048819 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405538 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049386 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405540 1074 "**verification:** $\\mathbf r\\prime(t)=\\langle-\\sin t,\\cos t,e^t\\rangle=\\mathbf F(\\cos t,\\sin t,e^t)$ for every real t.\n\nDifferentiate each coordinate of r and separately substitute r into F. Equality of all three components proves $\\mathbf r\\prime=\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r)$ on the stated domain." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405541 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406116 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405541 1071 "[Open in WeBWorK](https://webwork2.science.oregonstate.edu/webwork2/MTH_256_020_F2026/WebWork_1/6)\n\n**Question 6**\n\nFind the function $y(t)$ that satisfies the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}-2ty=15t^2e^{t^2}\n$$\n\nand the condition $y(0)=2$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405541 1071 "[Open in WeBWorK](https://webwork2.science.oregonstate.edu/webwork2/MTH_256_020_F2026/WebWork_1/6)\n\n**Question 6**\n\nFind the function $y(t)$ that satisfies the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}-2ty=15t^2e^{t^2}\n$$\n\nand the condition $y(0)=2$.\n\n$y(t)=$ {{field-1}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405541 1074 "Multiply by $e^{-t^2}$ to get $(e^{-t^2}y)\\prime=15t^2$. Integration gives $e^{-t^2}y=5t^3+C$, and $y(0)=2$ gives $C=2$. Differentiating the final expression cancels the $2ty$ term and leaves $15t^2e^{t^2}$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405542 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406102 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405542 1071 "Source question: 9.\n\nFind $\\iiint_E(x+3)\\,dV$ inside $x^2/16+y^2/25+z^2/36=1$. Use $u=x/4$, $v=y/5$, $w=z/6$.\n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405542 1071 "Source question: 9.\n\nFind $\\iiint_E(x+3)\\,dV$ inside $x^2/16+y^2/25+z^2/36=1$. Use $u=x/4$, $v=y/5$, $w=z/6$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405542 1074 "The ellipsoid becomes the unit ball. The inverse map (4u,5v,6w) has determinant 120, so integrate $120(4u+3)$. The odd u term vanishes; the constant contributes $360\\cdot4\\pi/3=480\\pi$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405543 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050149 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405543 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050891 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405543 1089 #edb/ref 17592186051090 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405544 1074 "The scalar triple product expands to $w_1(u_2v_3-u_3v_2)+w_2(u_3v_1-u_1v_3)+w_3(u_1v_2-u_2v_1)$. This is the first-row cofactor expansion of the determinant with rows $\\mathbf w,\\mathbf u,\\mathbf v$. Taking its absolute value gives the volume from Part B.\n\nUnlike the determinant notation in Problem 1, all entries here are scalars, so the determinant is a scalar. Check the alternating cofactor signs and retain the outer absolute value." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405547 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049536 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405547 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049645 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405548 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049768 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405548 1089 #edb/ref 17592186051091 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405549 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406247 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405549 1071 "Source question: 13.\n\nA thin wire forms the helix $x=6\\cos t,\\ y=6\\sin t,\\ z=t$. Find the mass M of four turns with constant density $\\rho$.\n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405549 1071 "Source question: 13.\n\nA thin wire forms the helix $x=6\\cos t,\\ y=6\\sin t,\\ z=t$. Find the mass M of four turns with constant density $\\rho$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405549 1074 "The speed is $\\sqrt{36\\sin^2t+36\\cos^2t+1}=\\sqrt{37}$. Four turns span 8π in t. Thus mass equals density times length: $\\rho\\sqrt{37}(8\\pi)$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405550 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406219 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405550 1071 "**Question 2 — Part D**\n\n$y'(x)=\\dfrac{1}{x},\\quad y(1)=3$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405550 1071 "**Question 2 — Part D**\n\n$y'(x)=\\dfrac{1}{x},\\quad y(1)=3$.\n\n$y(x)=$ {{field-1}}, on $x>0$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405550 1074 "Integrate to $\\ln|x|+C$. The interval containing the initial point $x=1$ is positive, so use $\\ln x$. At $1$, the logarithm is zero and $C=3$. The solution cannot cross the singular point $x=0$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405551 1074 "Drop a perpendicular from the tip of $\\mathbf v$ to the line along $\\mathbf u$. In the resulting right triangle, $\\sin\\theta=h/\\lVert\\mathbf v\\rVert$ for the acute angle shown. Thus $h=\\lVert\\mathbf v\\rVert\\sin\\theta$. For an obtuse included angle, the right triangle uses $\\pi-\\theta$ and gives the same result.\n\nThe height is the component of $\\mathbf v$ perpendicular to the base, not its full length. Identify the hypotenuse and opposite side in your triangle; $\\sin(\\pi-\\theta)=\\sin\\theta$ covers an obtuse included angle." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405553 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048960 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405553 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049859 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405558 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050295 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405558 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050866 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405559 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406170 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405559 1071 "Source question: 10.\n\nFind the curl of $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=\\langle3y+z,4z+9x,5z+6y+6x\\rangle$. \n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405559 1071 "Source question: 10.\n\nFind the curl of $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=\\langle3y+z,4z+9x,5z+6y+6x\\rangle$. \n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405559 1074 "The curl components are $R_y-Q_z=6-4=2$, $P_z-R_x=1-6=-5$, and $Q_x-P_y=9-3=6$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405560 1074 "Draw a short horizontal segment near $(0,0)$ and label it “not an integral curve.” The supplied field is nearly vertical in that neighborhood, so the segment visibly cuts across the direction marks.\n\nThe task only needs one counterexample. Choose a region where the mismatch between the segment’s tangent and the field is clear; no inferred differential equation is necessary." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405561 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049487 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405561 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050537 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405561 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050866 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405562 1074 "The product $e^xy$ is constant: $e^xy=C$.\n\nA differentiable function with derivative zero throughout an interval is constant on that interval. That constant is determined by the initial condition, not assumed to be zero." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405565 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048152 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405565 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049536 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405566 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406208 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405566 1071 "Source question: 7.\n\nFind $\\oint_C\\frac{y\\,dx-x\\,dy}{x^2+y^2}$ about $C:(x-8)^2+(y+7)^2=16$. Enter DNE if it does not exist.\n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405566 1071 "Source question: 7.\n\nFind $\\oint_C\\frac{y\\,dx-x\\,dy}{x^2+y^2}$ about $C:(x-8)^2+(y+7)^2=16$. Enter DNE if it does not exist.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405566 1074 "The disk has center (8,−7) and radius 4. The origin is outside, since its distance from the center is √113>4. The field is smooth on the disk and $Q_x-P_y=0$ there, so Green’s theorem gives zero. This value is unchanged by reversing orientation." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405567 1068 #edb/ref 17592186405998 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405567 1071 "Source question: 7.\n\nFind the curl of $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=\\langle e^{2x},3x^8y,6e^z\\rangle$. \n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405567 1071 "Source question: 7.\n\nFind the curl of $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=\\langle e^{2x},3x^8y,6e^z\\rangle$. \n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405567 1074 "The first two curl components vanish. The third is $Q_x-P_y=24x^7y-0$. The x derivative of the first component belongs to divergence, not curl." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405568 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406332 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405568 1071 "Source exercise: **1.2.2**, [printed page 42 (PDF page 54)](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F341%2FKuttler-LinearAlgebra-AFirstCourse-2017A.pdf#page=54).\n\n**Question 5**\n\nFind the point of intersection of the two lines\n\n$$\n3x+y=3 \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad x+2y=1.\n$$\n\nEnter the exact ordered pair $(x,y)$: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405568 1071 "Source exercise: **1.2.2**, [printed page 42 (PDF page 54)](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F341%2FKuttler-LinearAlgebra-AFirstCourse-2017A.pdf#page=54).\n\n**Question 5**\n\nFind the point of intersection of the two lines\n\n$$\n3x+y=3 \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad x+2y=1.\n$$\n\nEnter the exact ordered pair $(x,y)$: {{field-1}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405568 1074 "Eliminate $y$ to find the point satisfying both equations. Twice the first equation is $6x+2y=6$; subtracting $x+2y=1$ gives $5x=5$. Hence $x=1$, and $y=3-3x=0$. The point $(1,0)$ satisfies $3x+y=3$ and $x+2y=1$. A zero coordinate is valid: it means this intersection lies on the x-axis." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405570 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406243 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405570 1071 "Source question: 1.\n\nFind $\\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf F$ for $\\mathbf F=\\langle5x^2,4y^2,9z^6\\rangle$.\n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405570 1071 "Source question: 1.\n\nFind $\\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf F$ for $\\mathbf F=\\langle5x^2,4y^2,9z^6\\rangle$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405570 1074 "Divergence adds P_x+Q_y+R_z. Differentiate each component with respect to its matching coordinate: 10x, 8y, and 54z⁵." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405571 1074 "$\\frac{d}{dx}(\\sin x\\,y(x))=\\cos x\\,y(x)+\\sin x\\,y\\prime(x)$.\n\nTreat $y(x)$ as a differentiable function, not a constant. Differentiate each factor once and retain the undifferentiated partner in each term." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405573 1074 "**tangent-and-orthogonal:** Tangent: $(\\pm2,0),(0,\\pm2)$. Orthogonal: all four $(\\pm\\sqrt2,\\pm\\sqrt2)$ with signs independent.\n\nUse the nonzero tangent $T=(-y,x)$. Tangency requires the determinant $2xy=0$; intersect with the circle to get its four axis intercepts. Orthogonality requires $F\\cdot T=x^2-y^2=0$; then x²=y²=2." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405581 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406125 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405581 1071 "[Open in WeBWorK](https://webwork2.science.oregonstate.edu/webwork2/MTH_256_020_F2026/WebWork_1/1)\n\n**Question 1**\n\nFind the function satisfying the differential equation\n\n$$\ny'-2y=4e^{4t}\n$$\n\nand $y(0)=0$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405581 1071 "[Open in WeBWorK](https://webwork2.science.oregonstate.edu/webwork2/MTH_256_020_F2026/WebWork_1/1)\n\n**Question 1**\n\nFind the function satisfying the differential equation\n\n$$\ny'-2y=4e^{4t}\n$$\n\nand $y(0)=0$.\n\n$y(t)=$ {{field-1}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405581 1074 "The integrating factor is $e^{-2t}$, so $(e^{-2t}y)\\prime=4e^{2t}$. Integrating gives $e^{-2t}y=2e^{2t}+C$. At $0$, $0=2+C$, hence $C=-2$. Differentiation gives $y\\prime-2y=4e^{4t}$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405583 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406200 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405583 1071 "Source question: 6.\n\nEvaluate $\\oint_C(4x^3-2x^2y)dx+9xy^2dy$ counterclockwise around the region bounded by $y=x^2$ and $x=y^2$.\n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405583 1071 "Source question: 6.\n\nEvaluate $\\oint_C(4x^3-2x^2y)dx+9xy^2dy$ counterclockwise around the region bounded by $y=x^2$ and $x=y^2$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405583 1074 "$Q_x-P_y=9y^2+2x^2$. Integrate over 0≤x≤1, x²≤y≤√x. Symmetry under swapping x and y makes both second moments equal: $\\iint_Rx^2dA=\\int_0^1(x^{5/2}-x^4)dx=3/35$. Thus the result is (9+2)3/35." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405585 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049465 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405588 1068 #edb/ref 17592186405920 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405588 1071 "Source question: 9.\n\nFind $\\int_C6x\\cos y\\,dx-8y\\sin x\\,dy$ along the segments (0,0) → (2,0) → (2,2) → (0,2), in that order.\n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405588 1071 "Source question: 9.\n\nFind $\\int_C6x\\cos y\\,dx-8y\\sin x\\,dy$ along the segments (0,0) → (2,0) → (2,2) → (0,2), in that order.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405588 1074 "The bottom segment contributes $\\int_0^2 6x dx=12$. The right segment contributes $\\int_0^2-8y\\sin2\\,dy=-16\\sin2$. The top, traversed right to left, contributes $\\int_2^0 6x\\cos2\\,dx=-12\\cos2$. Do not add a fourth segment." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405589 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049114 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405589 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049138 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405589 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050155 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405590 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406214 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405590 1071 "Source question: 8.\n\nFind the curl of $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=\\langle6\\cos(4x),5\\sin y,9e^{4xz}\\rangle$. \n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405590 1071 "Source question: 8.\n\nFind the curl of $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=\\langle6\\cos(4x),5\\sin y,9e^{4xz}\\rangle$. \n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405590 1074 "Only $R_x=36ze^{4xz}$ contributes. The middle component is $P_z-R_x=-36ze^{4xz}$; preserve that minus sign." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405592 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050149 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405592 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050875 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405596 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048259 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405601 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050989 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405603 1068 #edb/ref 17592186405884 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405603 1071 "**Question 2 — Part D**\n\nFind the arc length from $t=0$ to $t=\\frac{\\pi}{3}$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405603 1071 "**Question 2 — Part D**\n\nFind the arc length from $t=0$ to $t=\\frac{\\pi}{3}$.\n\nArc length: {{field-1}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405603 1074 "$L=\\int_0^{\\pi/3}5\\sec t\\,dt=5[\\ln|\\sec t+\\tan t|]_0^{\\pi/3}=5\\ln(2+\\sqrt3)$. The integrand is positive throughout the interval." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405609 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049465 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405611 1064 "**6.3 Conservative Vector Fields**\n\nDue: 2026-10-22\nSource: [Macmillan Achieve](https://achieve.macmillanlearning.com/ihub/assessment/f188d950-dd73-11e0-9572-0800200c9a66/5a1db2a7-4295-4a4c-9dbb-ceb8b174a282/3a627574-021f-4d0f-9969-54703a59bf12)" 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405611 1064 "**6.3 Conservative Vector Fields**\n\nDue: 2026-10-22\nSource: [Macmillan Achieve](https://achieve.macmillanlearning.com/ihub/assessment/f188d950-dd73-11e0-9572-0800200c9a66/5a1db2a7-4295-4a4c-9dbb-ceb8b174a282/3a627574-021f-4d0f-9969-54703a59bf12)\n\nStudy answers and explanations are authored additions, not publisher solutions.\n\n## Instructions\n\nGive exact answers, using symbolic notation and fractions where needed. Preserve K or C as requested for constants. Questions 1–4 explicitly require the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals. Source: Sullivan 2e Calculus, W.H. Freeman. The platform allows unlimited attempts with no incorrect-attempt penalty; submissions up to 50 days late incur a 2% daily penalty." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405612 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048225 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405612 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049050 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405613 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406244 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405613 1071 "**(b)** $\\mathbf F=\\frac{\\langle x,y\\rangle}{\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}\\mathbf i+\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}\\mathbf j$.\n\nPolar form: ____. Explain how the result agrees with the field plot." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405613 1071 "**(b)** $\\mathbf F=\\frac{\\langle x,y\\rangle}{\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}\\mathbf i+\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}\\mathbf j$.\n\nPolar form: {{polar}}. Explain how the result agrees with the field plot." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405613 1074 "**interpretation:** Divide the result of (a) by r for r>0. It is radial outward with constant unit length and is undefined at the origin.\n\nDivide the result of (a) by r for r>0. It is radial outward with constant unit length and is undefined at the origin. Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$ and the basis identities from 4(b). Polar directions require r>0; in (a) and (c), the Cartesian field extends to the zero vector at the origin." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405614 1074 "The vector $\\mathbf u\\times\\mathbf v$ is normal to the base. With unit normal $\\mathbf n=(\\mathbf u\\times\\mathbf v)/\\lVert\\mathbf u\\times\\mathbf v\\rVert$, the projection of $\\mathbf w$ on that normal is $\\mathbf w\\cdot\\mathbf n=\\lVert\\mathbf w\\rVert\\cos\\theta$. For the acute angle shown, this projection is the height. For arbitrary orientation, geometric height is $\\lVert\\mathbf w\\rVert|\\cos\\theta|$.\n\nHere the angle is measured from the normal, so use cosine. The worksheet formula assumes the acute angle drawn; an obtuse angle gives a negative signed projection, whose absolute value is the physical height. Assume a nondegenerate base when defining its unit normal." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405615 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406061 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405615 1071 "[Open in WeBWorK](https://webwork2.science.oregonstate.edu/webwork2/MTH_256_020_F2026/WebWork_1/3)\n\n**Question 3**\n\nFind the particular solution of the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+y\\cos(x)=5\\cos(x)\n$$\n\nsatisfying the initial condition $y(0)=7$.\n\nYour answer should be a function of $x$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405615 1071 "[Open in WeBWorK](https://webwork2.science.oregonstate.edu/webwork2/MTH_256_020_F2026/WebWork_1/3)\n\n**Question 3**\n\nFind the particular solution of the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+y\\cos(x)=5\\cos(x)\n$$\n\nsatisfying the initial condition $y(0)=7$.\n\nYour answer should be a function of $x$.\n\n$y(x)=$ {{field-1}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405615 1074 "Let $u=y-5$. Then $u\\prime=-u\\cos x$, so $u=Ce^{-\\sin x}$. The initial value gives $C=2$. Check $y\\prime=-2\\cos x\\,e^{-\\sin x}$ and $y\\cos x=5\\cos x+2\\cos x\\,e^{-\\sin x}$; their sum is $5\\cos x$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405616 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050295 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405616 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050866 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405617 1068 #edb/ref 17592186405981 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405617 1071 "Source question: 6.\n\nFor $\\mathbf F=\\langle-y,x,z\\rangle$ on $S:x^2+y^2+z^2=49$, z≥0, find $\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,dS$ with outer unit normal n.\n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405617 1071 "Source question: 6.\n\nFor $\\mathbf F=\\langle-y,x,z\\rangle$ on $S:x^2+y^2+z^2=49$, z≥0, find $\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,dS$ with outer unit normal n.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405617 1074 "On the radius-7 sphere, $n=(x,y,z)/7$, so F·n=z²/7. Use z=7cosφ and dS=49sinφ dφdθ, 0≤φ≤π/2: flux $=343(2\\pi)\\int_0^{\\pi/2}\\cos^2\\phi\\sin\\phi d\\phi=686\\pi/3$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405619 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048225 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405619 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050488 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405620 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048225 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405620 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050488 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405622 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406006 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405622 1071 "Source question: 7.\n\n**Question 7**\n\nFind the arc length for $x(t)=13t^2+1$, $y(t)=2t^3-1$, $0\\le t\\le2$. Give an exact answer.\n\n$s=$ ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405622 1071 "Source question: 7.\n\n**Question 7**\n\nFind the arc length for $x(t)=13t^2+1$, $y(t)=2t^3-1$, $0\\le t\\le2$. Give an exact answer.\n\n$s=$ {{field-1}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405622 1074 "The speed is $\\sqrt{(26t)^2+(6t^2)^2}=2t\\sqrt{169+9t^2}$ on this interval. Substituting $u=169+9t^2$ gives $s=[2(169+9t^2)^{3/2}/27]_0^2=2(205^{3/2}-2197)/27$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405623 1068 #edb/ref 17592186405962 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405623 1071 "Source question: 6.\n\nFor the conservative field $\\mathbf F=\\langle3x-\\cos y,x\\sin y\\rangle$, evaluate $\\int_C(3x-\\cos y)dx+x\\sin y\\,dy$ from $(1,\\pi/2)$ to $(2,\\pi)$ along a piecewise smooth curve.\n\nIntegral: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405623 1071 "Source question: 6.\n\nFor the conservative field $\\mathbf F=\\langle3x-\\cos y,x\\sin y\\rangle$, evaluate $\\int_C(3x-\\cos y)dx+x\\sin y\\,dy$ from $(1,\\pi/2)$ to $(2,\\pi)$ along a piecewise smooth curve.\n\nIntegral: {{integral}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405623 1074 "A potential is $f=3x^2/2-x\\cos y$. Its gradient is the stated field. The endpoint values are 8 and 3/2, giving 13/2." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405624 1074 "Draw and label the upper hyperbola branch $y=\\sqrt{x^2+1/4}$. Draw the visible arc for $|x|<\\sqrt3/2$, within the supplied plotting window. The function is a solution for every real $x$: $y\\prime=x/\\sqrt{x^2+1/4}=x/y$. It stays above the singular line $y=0$.\n\n![Authored reference graph](images/7f/7f0bf50470f063b2555f5ac202e8bb1ef934ac29d9fc7bf4726d2d79de612da6.png)\n\nThe curve must be tangent to the field at each point and must be a single-valued differentiable graph on an interval. A branch with $y\ne0$ avoids the undefined right-hand side." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405625 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406004 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405625 1071 "Source question: 9.\n\nA fluid of constant mass density crosses $z=81-x^2-y^2$, z≥0, with velocity $\\mathbf F=\\langle x,y,11\\rangle$. Find the mass flowing outward in one unit of time.\n\n**Source gap:** The constant density has no numerical value. For this study answer, denote it by $\\rho_0$; report signed outward mass flow.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405625 1071 "Source question: 9.\n\nA fluid of constant mass density crosses $z=81-x^2-y^2$, z≥0, with velocity $\\mathbf F=\\langle x,y,11\\rangle$. Find the mass flowing outward in one unit of time.\n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405625 1074 "Outward relative to the solid beneath the paraboloid and above z=0 is upward: $n\\,dS=(2x,2y,1)dxdy$. The flux density is 2r²+11. Integrating over r≤9 gives $2\\pi\\int_0^9(2r^2+11)rdr=\\pi(9^4+11\\cdot9^2)=7452\\pi$. Multiply by ρ₀; with unit density omit that factor. No density value is supplied in the source." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405626 1089 #edb/ref 17592186051076 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405627 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406015 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405627 1071 "Source question: 11.\n\nFind the lateral area above the xy-plane and below $z=3-y^2$, formed by vertical segments from $C: x=\\sin(11t),\\ y=\\cos(11t),\\ 0\\le t\\le2\\pi/11$.\n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405627 1071 "Source question: 11.\n\nFind the lateral area above the xy-plane and below $z=3-y^2$, formed by vertical segments from $C: x=\\sin(11t),\\ y=\\cos(11t),\\ 0\\le t\\le2\\pi/11$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405627 1074 "Area is height integrated over the base arc: $A=\\int_C(3-y^2)ds$. The speed is 11; u=11t gives $\\int_0^{2\\pi}(3-\\cos^2u)du=6\\pi-\\pi$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405632 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048832 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405632 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050196 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405634 1068 #edb/ref 17592186405919 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405634 1071 "Source question: 9.\n\nThe field $\\mathbf F=\\langle ye^{9x},e^{9x}/9\\rangle$ is conservative. Find a potential f. Use K for the arbitrary constant and absorb into K as much as possible.\n\n$f(x,y)=$ ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405634 1071 "Source question: 9.\n\nThe field $\\mathbf F=\\langle ye^{9x},e^{9x}/9\\rangle$ is conservative. Find a potential f. Use K for the arbitrary constant and absorb into K as much as possible.\n\n$f(x,y)=$ {{potential}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405634 1074 "Integrate ye⁹ˣ with respect to x: $f=ye^{9x}/9+g(y)$. Its y derivative is e⁹ˣ/9+g′, so g′=0. Differentiating in x cancels the factor 1/9." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405637 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050418 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405639 1068 #edb/ref 17592186405972 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405639 1071 "Source question: 8.\n\n**Question 8**\n\nChoose the graph of $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=x\\mathbf i$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405639 1071 "Source question: 8.\n\n**Question 8**\n\nChoose the graph of $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=x\\mathbf i$.\n\n1. ![Choice 1](images/97/9724974f21977c050a42251a25487efa8273e9164369e2bb08b77aa537ed0ba6.png)\n\n2. ![Choice 2](images/6c/6c1abbaeb1eaf80867292350ed5a0750966cce2242c24d67e69ee2546f7ca050.png)\n\n3. ![Choice 3](images/1b/1b9471c36998e10bc10619591c03d729542d76c63538dcf2e0b52abc0ae1244e.png)\n\n4. ![Choice 4](images/c2/c2bb647a4098f3706ad9f90fcca52103d9c5b95d8e7c6b5d01a0de49cadf473b.png)" 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405639 1074 "These horizontal arrows point away from $x=0$, matching $\\mathbf F=\\langle x,0,0\\rangle$. The direction reverses with the sign of $x$, the length is $|x|$, and changing $y$ or $z$ does not change the vector." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405640 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050514 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405641 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048923 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405641 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049366 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405641 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050033 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405641 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050149 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405644 1074 "**drawing:** Draw the radius-a circle centered at the origin. At every nonzero point, draw an equal-length arrow pointing radially outward; no field vector exists at the origin. At (a,0), (0,a), (−a,0), (0,−a), arrows point right, up, left, down.\n\n![Authored radial field reference](images/a8/a81e47b17c286f2bbb3393045774604a075906fc99c8a42c6562c95605d6c64b.png)\n\nNormalization makes every field vector length 1. Its direction is the position vector (x,y). The reference diagram uses uniformly scaled arrows and labels distance in units of a." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405646 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048960 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405646 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049366 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405646 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050142 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405648 1068 #edb/ref 17592186405895 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405648 1071 "Source question: 19.\n\nFind the work of $\\mathbf F=\\langle5x-2y,6xy\\rangle$ along $\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle2\\cos t,3\\sin t\\rangle$ from t=0 to t=π/2.\n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405648 1071 "Source question: 19.\n\nFind the work of $\\mathbf F=\\langle5x-2y,6xy\\rangle$ along $\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle2\\cos t,3\\sin t\\rangle$ from t=0 to t=π/2.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405648 1074 "The dot product is $-20\\sin t\\cos t+12\\sin^2t+108\\sin t\\cos^2t$. Its three integrals are −10, 3π, and 36, respectively." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405651 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406095 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405651 1071 "Source question: 4.\n\n**Question 4**\n\nChoose the graph of $\\mathbf F(x,y)=y\\mathbf i-7\\mathbf j$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405651 1071 "Source question: 4.\n\n**Question 4**\n\nChoose the graph of $\\mathbf F(x,y)=y\\mathbf i-7\\mathbf j$.\n\n1. ![Choice 1](images/be/be65a63c2aee91354eedac9d31afe2f83e263c50ffda0230c7b785300bc8ee3a.png)\n\n2. ![Choice 2](images/e3/e3f1c76198fa480b7bf0f9384d3fffed3476133067a66c0207c49d2d5c3c2e7f.png)\n\n3. ![Choice 3](images/0b/0bab9adaea6167a7be7ad6c342255105b85698df9f9445246f312e24e79c66a5.png)\n\n4. ![Choice 4](images/61/6137603d73463b9383e474366f28cf5897065e7b5afd53b7c019c7be50df50f5.png)" 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405651 1074 "The component $y$ points right above the $x$-axis and left below it; the component $-7$ always points down. Along each horizontal row the arrows agree, matching this graph." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405652 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406269 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405652 1071 "Source question: 2.\n\nFind $\\nabla\\times\\mathbf F$ for $\\mathbf F=\\langle6xyz^9,y^2\\sin(6z),3xe^{6z}\\rangle$.\n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405652 1071 "Source question: 2.\n\nFind $\\nabla\\times\\mathbf F$ for $\\mathbf F=\\langle6xyz^9,y^2\\sin(6z),3xe^{6z}\\rangle$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405652 1074 "Use (R_y−Q_z,P_z−R_x,Q_x−P_y). The pairs of derivatives are (0,6y²cos(6z)), (54xyz⁸,3e⁶ᶻ), and (0,6xz⁹). The second component is P_z−R_x, so keep that subtraction order." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405654 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406160 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405654 1071 "Source question: 5.\n\nEvaluate $\\oint_C(5x^4-5y^2)dx+(7y-4xy)dy$ counterclockwise around the region bounded by $y=x^2$ and $y=\\sqrt x$.\n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405654 1071 "Source question: 5.\n\nEvaluate $\\oint_C(5x^4-5y^2)dx+(7y-4xy)dy$ counterclockwise around the region bounded by $y=x^2$ and $y=\\sqrt x$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405654 1074 "The curves intersect at x=0,1 and x²≤y≤√x. Since $Q_x-P_y=-4y+10y=6y$, the integral is $\\int_0^1\\int_{x^2}^{\\sqrt x}6y\\,dy\\,dx=3\\int_0^1(x-x^4)dx=9/10$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405655 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048960 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405655 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049859 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405657 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049465 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405657 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050540 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405659 1068 #edb/ref 17592186405860 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405659 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406174 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405659 1071 "**(a)** $\\phi=e^x\\sin y$. Find its gradient field and the points excluded from its domain.\n\nGradient: ____.\n\nExcluded points: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405659 1071 "**(a)** $\\phi=e^x\\sin y$. Find its gradient field and the points excluded from its domain.\n\nGradient: {{gradient}}.\n\nExcluded points: {{excluded}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405659 1074 "Differentiate in x while holding y fixed, then in y holding x fixed. Exponential and trigonometric functions and their derivatives exist everywhere." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405660 1068 #edb/ref 17592186405851 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405660 1071 "Source question: 9.\n\nDetermine whether the following force is a conservative vector field.\n\n$\\mathbf F=\\langle6x,9y,0\\rangle$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405660 1071 "Source question: 9.\n\nDetermine whether the following force is a conservative vector field.\n\n$\\mathbf F=\\langle6x,9y,0\\rangle$.\n\n1. The field is conservative.\n\n2. The field is not conservative." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405660 1074 "The potential f=3x²+(9/2)y² has gradient F throughout space. Thus F is conservative; its curl is also identically zero." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405662 1074 "**proof:** C is the level curve $\\eta=a^2$. Since $\\nabla\\eta\\ne0$ on C, it is normal to C. There $F=\\nabla\\eta/(2a)$, so F is also normal to C.\n\nThe condition a>0 excludes the origin. Multiplying a nonzero normal vector by the positive scalar 1/(2a) preserves its normal direction." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405664 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406318 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405664 1071 "**(b)** Find $\\nabla\\eta(x,y)$. Notice that η is not a potential for F, but its gradient is parallel to F and points in the same direction.\n\nGradient: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405664 1071 "**(b)** Find $\\nabla\\eta(x,y)$. Notice that η is not a potential for F, but its gradient is parallel to F and points in the same direction.\n\nGradient: {{gradient}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405664 1074 "$\\eta_x=2x$ and $\\eta_y=2y$, so $\\nabla\\eta=2\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}\\,F$ at every nonzero point. The scalar is positive; it is not identically 1, so η is not a potential for F." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405665 1074 "One pair is $\\mathbf u=\\langle0,1,0\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf v=\\langle4/5,0,-3/5\\rangle$. They are unit vectors, $\\mathbf u\\cdot\\mathbf v=0$, and each has dot product zero with $\\nabla V(P)=\\langle12,0,16\\rangle$. Other orthogonal unit pairs in this plane are valid.\n\nZero change means $12u_x+16u_z=0$. Choose two independent directions in that plane, then check their mutual orthogonality as well as their orthogonality to the gradient. Merely choosing a vector and its negative does not give an orthogonal pair." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405666 1089 #edb/ref 17592186051091 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405667 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048259 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405668 1068 #edb/ref 17592186405901 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405668 1071 "**(d)** $\\mathbf F=\\frac{\\langle-y,x\\rangle}{\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}=\\frac{-y}{\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}\\mathbf i+\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}\\mathbf j$.\n\nPolar form: ____. Explain how the result agrees with the field plot." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405668 1071 "**(d)** $\\mathbf F=\\frac{\\langle-y,x\\rangle}{\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}=\\frac{-y}{\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}\\mathbf i+\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}\\mathbf j$.\n\nPolar form: {{polar}}. Explain how the result agrees with the field plot." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405668 1074 "**interpretation:** Divide (c) by r for r>0. The field follows increasing angle with unit length and is undefined at the origin.\n\nDivide (c) by r for r>0. The field follows increasing angle with unit length and is undefined at the origin. Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$ and the basis identities from 4(b). Polar directions require r>0; in (a) and (c), the Cartesian field extends to the zero vector at the origin." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405671 1068 #edb/ref 17592186405903 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405671 1071 "**Question 3 — Part C**\n\nIf $y(0)=y_0$, what is $e^0y(0)$ equal to?" 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405671 1071 "**Question 3 — Part C**\n\nIf $y(0)=y_0$, what is $e^0y(0)$ equal to?\n\n$e^0y(0)=$ {{field-1}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405671 1074 "Since $e^0=1$ and $y(0)=y_0$, the constant value of $e^xy$ is $y_0$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405672 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406177 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405672 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406328 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405672 1071 "Source question: 8.\n\nFor $f(x,y)=3xe^y$, find its line integrals with respect to x and y along $x=e^{7y}$ from (1,0) to $(e^7,1)$.\n\n$\\int_C f\\,dx=$ ____.\n\n$\\int_C f\\,dy=$ ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405672 1071 "Source question: 8.\n\nFor $f(x,y)=3xe^y$, find its line integrals with respect to x and y along $x=e^{7y}$ from (1,0) to $(e^7,1)$.\n\n$\\int_C f\\,dx=$ {{dx}}.\n\n$\\int_C f\\,dy=$ {{dy}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405672 1074 "Parameterize by y from 0 to 1. Then f=3e^{8y} and dx=7e^{7y}dy. The dx integral is $\\int_0^1 21e^{15y}dy$; the dy integral is $\\int_0^1 3e^{8y}dy$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405675 1068 #edb/ref 17592186405916 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405675 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406286 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405675 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406309 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405675 1071 "**Question 3 — Part B**\n\nUse polar coordinates to evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_0^\\infty\\int_0^\\infty e^{-x^2-y^2}\\,dy\\,dx.\n$$\n\nWhat does this say about $\\displaystyle\\int_0^\\infty e^{-x^2}\\,dx$? How about $\\displaystyle\\int_{-\\infty}^\\infty e^{-x^2}\\,dx$?" 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405675 1071 "**Question 3 — Part B**\n\nUse polar coordinates to evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_0^\\infty\\int_0^\\infty e^{-x^2-y^2}\\,dy\\,dx.\n$$\n\nWhat does this say about $\\displaystyle\\int_0^\\infty e^{-x^2}\\,dx$? How about $\\displaystyle\\int_{-\\infty}^\\infty e^{-x^2}\\,dx$?\n\nDouble integral: {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\int_0^\\infty e^{-x^2}\\,dx=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\int_{-\\infty}^\\infty e^{-x^2}\\,dx=$ {{field-3}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405675 1074 "The first quadrant becomes $0\\le\\theta\\le\\pi/2$, $r\\ge0$. The double integral is $\\int_0^{\\pi/2}\\int_0^\\infty e^{-r^2}r\\,dr\\,d\\theta=(\\pi/2)(1/2)=\\pi/4$. Because the nonnegative integrand factors as $e^{-x^2}e^{-y^2}$, this equals $I^2$ where $I=\\int_0^\\infty e^{-x^2}\\,dx>0$. Hence $I=\\sqrt\\pi/2$. Evenness doubles it on the real line." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405676 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406194 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405676 1071 "**Question 4 — Part A**\n\nPoints with horizontal tangents: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405676 1071 "**Question 4 — Part A**\n\nPoints with horizontal tangents: {{field-1}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405676 1074 "A horizontal tangent requires $y'=0$ and $x'\\ne0$. Here $y'=4t$ vanishes at $t=0$, while $x'=3t^2-144=-144$. The point is $(0,0)$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405679 1089 #edb/ref 17592186051076 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405683 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406167 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405683 1071 "Source question: 9.\n\n**Question 9**\n\nFind the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)=xe^{8y}$. Use component form or standard basis vectors, with exact values.\n\n$\\nabla f(x,y)=$ ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405683 1071 "Source question: 9.\n\n**Question 9**\n\nFind the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)=xe^{8y}$. Use component form or standard basis vectors, with exact values.\n\n$\\nabla f(x,y)=$ {{field-1}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405683 1074 "The gradient lists partial derivatives. Holding $y$ fixed gives $f_x=e^{8y}$; holding $x$ fixed and using the chain rule gives $f_y=8xe^{8y}$. Thus $\\nabla f=\\langle e^{8y},8xe^{8y}\\rangle$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405684 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048191 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405686 1068 #edb/ref 17592186405899 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405686 1071 "Source question: 7.\n\nUse Stokes’ theorem to find $\\oint_C[2x\\,dx+(3x+y)dy+(6x+y+z)dz]$, where $\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle7\\cos t,7\\sin t,7\\rangle$, 0≤t≤2π, counterclockwise.\n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405686 1071 "Source question: 7.\n\nUse Stokes’ theorem to find $\\oint_C[2x\\,dx+(3x+y)dy+(6x+y+z)dz]$, where $\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle7\\cos t,7\\sin t,7\\rangle$, 0≤t≤2π, counterclockwise.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405686 1074 "The curl of (2x,3x+y,6x+y+z) is (1,−6,3). Use the horizontal radius-7 disk with upward normal. Only its third component contributes: 3·π·7²=147π." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405691 1089 #edb/ref 17592186051076 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405693 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050295 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405693 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050866 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405694 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406126 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405694 1071 "Source question: 12.\n\nFind $\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,dS$ for $\\mathbf F=\\langle8z,3x,6y\\rangle$ on the hemisphere $z=\\sqrt{1-x^2-y^2}$.\n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405694 1071 "Source question: 12.\n\nFind $\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,dS$ for $\\mathbf F=\\langle8z,3x,6y\\rangle$ on the hemisphere $z=\\sqrt{1-x^2-y^2}$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405694 1074 "For the outward normal on the unit sphere, F·n=8xz+3xy+6yz. Each term integrates to zero by reflection symmetry: xz and xy change sign under x→−x; yz changes sign under y→−y. Thus flux is 0. The source omits orientation, but reversing the normal leaves this zero answer unchanged. A closed-surface check: div F=0 and the closing disk has flux ∫∫−6y dA=0." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405696 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049090 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405696 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049487 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405696 1089 #edb/ref 17592186051022 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405698 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050149 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405698 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050891 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405701 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050149 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405701 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050875 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405702 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406248 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405702 1071 "Source question: 8.\n\nFind $\\iint_R7(x+y)\\sin(x-y)\\,dA$ on the triangle with vertices (−5,5), (5,5), (0,0). Use $u=x+y$, $v=x-y$.\n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405702 1071 "Source question: 8.\n\nFind $\\iint_R7(x+y)\\sin(x-y)\\,dA$ on the triangle with vertices (−5,5), (5,5), (0,0). Use $u=x+y$, $v=x-y$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405702 1074 "The inverse is $x=(u+v)/2$, $y=(u-v)/2$, so $|J|=1/2$. The transformed vertices are (0,−10),(10,0),(0,0), giving 0≤u≤10 and u−10≤v≤0. Hence $I=\\frac72\\int_0^{10}u[\\cos(u-10)-1]du=\\frac72[(1-\\cos10)-50]$. Angles are in radians." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405703 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406289 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405703 1071 "Source question: 10.\n\nA thin wire follows $x=9\\sin(2t),\\ y=9\\cos(2t),\\ 0\\le t\\le\\pi/4$ and has density $\\rho(x,y)=x+y+2$. Find its mass M.\n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405703 1071 "Source question: 10.\n\nA thin wire follows $x=9\\sin(2t),\\ y=9\\cos(2t),\\ 0\\le t\\le\\pi/4$ and has density $\\rho(x,y)=x+y+2$. Find its mass M.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405703 1074 "Mass is $\\int_C\\rho ds$. Speed is 18, so $M=18\\int_0^{\\pi/4}[9\\sin2t+9\\cos2t+2]dt=18(9+\\pi/2)$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405705 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049462 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405705 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049494 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405705 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049678 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405707 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048191 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405710 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406071 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405710 1071 "**(c)** C consists of the line segment(s) joining $(7\\pi,0)\\to(7\\pi,1)\\to(8\\pi,1)$, in that order.\n\nIntegral: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405710 1071 "**(c)** C consists of the line segment(s) joining $(7\\pi,0)\\to(7\\pi,1)\\to(8\\pi,1)$, in that order.\n\nIntegral: {{integral}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405710 1074 "The potential is $f=e^{2y}\\sin x$. Both endpoint values vanish, because sin(7π)=sin(8π)=0. Each path therefore has integral 0; intermediate contributions cancel." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405712 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406236 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405712 1071 "**Question 2 — Part A**\n\nComplete the rectangular equation: $y=$ ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405712 1071 "**Question 2 — Part A**\n\nComplete the rectangular equation: $y=$ {{field-1}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405712 1074 "Solve $x=8t+1$ for $t=(x-1)/8$. Substitution gives $y=(x-1)/8+8=(x+63)/8$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405713 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406072 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405713 1071 "Source question: 6.\n\n**Question 6**\n\nFind the arc length for $x(t)=7t^3$, $y(t)=8t^2$, $0\\le t\\le2$. Give an exact answer.\n\n$s=$ ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405713 1071 "Source question: 6.\n\n**Question 6**\n\nFind the arc length for $x(t)=7t^3$, $y(t)=8t^2$, $0\\le t\\le2$. Give an exact answer.\n\n$s=$ {{field-1}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405713 1074 "Arc length integrates speed: $s=\\int_0^2\\sqrt{(21t^2)^2+(16t)^2}\\,dt=\\int_0^2t\\sqrt{441t^2+256}\\,dt$, since $t\\ge0$. With $u=441t^2+256$, this is $[(441t^2+256)^{3/2}/1323]_0^2=(2020^{3/2}-4096)/1323$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405714 1089 #edb/ref 17592186051091 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405719 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406209 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405719 1071 "Source question: 1.\n\nEvaluate $\\oint_C3y\\,dx$ counterclockwise around the rectangle with vertices (0,0), (2,0), (2,6), (0,6).\n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405719 1071 "Source question: 1.\n\nEvaluate $\\oint_C3y\\,dx$ counterclockwise around the rectangle with vertices (0,0), (2,0), (2,6), (0,6).\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405719 1074 "$P=3y$, Q=0, so $Q_x-P_y=-3$. Green’s theorem gives −3 times the rectangle area 12, or −36. Directly, only the top horizontal side contributes: $\\int_2^0 18dx=-36$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405720 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048314 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405720 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050875 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405722 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048703 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405722 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050911 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405723 1068 #edb/ref 17592186405927 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405723 1071 "Source question: 17.\n\nFind the work of $\\mathbf F=\\langle7y,3x\\rangle$ along $\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t,t^2\\rangle$ from t=0 to t=1.\n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405723 1071 "Source question: 17.\n\nFind the work of $\\mathbf F=\\langle7y,3x\\rangle$ along $\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t,t^2\\rangle$ from t=0 to t=1.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405723 1074 "$F(r)=(7t^2,3t)$, $r\\prime=(1,2t)$. Thus $W=\\int_0^1(7t^2+6t^2)dt=13/3$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405725 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049465 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405728 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406090 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405728 1071 "Source question: 10.\n\n**Question 10**\n\nFind the gradient vector field of $f(x,y,z)=x^6y+4xyz^8$. Use component form or standard basis vectors, with exact values.\n\n$\\nabla f(x,y,z)=$ ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405728 1071 "Source question: 10.\n\n**Question 10**\n\nFind the gradient vector field of $f(x,y,z)=x^6y+4xyz^8$. Use component form or standard basis vectors, with exact values.\n\n$\\nabla f(x,y,z)=$ {{field-1}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405728 1074 "Differentiate with respect to one variable at a time, holding the others fixed: $f_x=6x^5y+4yz^8$, $f_y=x^6+4xz^8$, and $f_z=32xyz^7$. These are the three gradient components." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405730 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406275 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405730 1071 "Source question: 3.\n\nEvaluate $\\oint_C8y(x^2+y^2)dx-8x(x^2+y^2)dy$ counterclockwise around the unit circle $x^2+y^2=1$.\n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405730 1071 "Source question: 3.\n\nEvaluate $\\oint_C8y(x^2+y^2)dx-8x(x^2+y^2)dy$ counterclockwise around the unit circle $x^2+y^2=1$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405730 1074 "$Q_x-P_y=-32(x^2+y^2)$. Over the unit disk the double integral is $-32\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\int_0^1r^3drd\\theta=-16\\pi$. On the circle, direct substitution also gives the constant −8 in the θ integrand." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405731 1074 "Draw the unit circle and, for example, tangents at $(0,1)$ and $(1/\\sqrt2,1/\\sqrt2)$. The first tangent is $y=1$ with slope $0=-0/1$. The second is $y=-x+\\sqrt2$ with slope $-1=-(1/\\sqrt2)/(1/\\sqrt2)$. Both agree with $dy/dx=-x/y$. Other correctly drawn and checked tangent points are valid.\n\n![Authored reference graph](images/73/730c9f459fe4f68e944d2fa043f43b145dd3b0b237ebb20533b13aefd6bdacad.png)\n\nLabel each chosen point and tangent. A tangent to a circle is perpendicular to its radius, which provides a geometric check on the slope. If you choose $(\\pm1,0)$, use a vertical tangent and explain why $-x/y$ is undefined there." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405732 1068 #edb/ref 17592186405942 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405732 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406224 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405732 1071 "**(c)** $\\phi=\\frac\\pi2-\\tan^{-1}(x/y)$. Find its gradient field and the points excluded from its domain.\n\nGradient: ____.\n\nExcluded points: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405732 1071 "**(c)** $\\phi=\\frac\\pi2-\\tan^{-1}(x/y)$. Find its gradient field and the points excluded from its domain.\n\nGradient: {{gradient}}.\n\nExcluded points: {{excluded}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405732 1074 "Differentiate the negative arctangent: $\\phi_x=-(1/y)/(1+x^2/y^2)$ and $\\phi_y=(x/y^2)/(1+x^2/y^2)$. The gradient agrees with part (b) where both potentials are defined, but this potential requires y≠0." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405734 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048152 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405734 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049536 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405735 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048495 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405735 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048703 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405736 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048451 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405736 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048684 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405736 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048832 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405737 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049462 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405737 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049494 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405737 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049678 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405739 1089 #edb/ref 17592186051091 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405740 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406152 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405740 1071 "Source question: 16.\n\nFind $\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot d\\mathbf r$ for $\\mathbf F=\\langle e^x,e^{-y},e^{2z}\\rangle$, $\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle5t,t^2,t^3\\rangle$, $0\\le t\\le1$.\n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405740 1071 "Source question: 16.\n\nFind $\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot d\\mathbf r$ for $\\mathbf F=\\langle e^x,e^{-y},e^{2z}\\rangle$, $\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle5t,t^2,t^3\\rangle$, $0\\le t\\le1$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405740 1074 "The dot product is $5e^{5t}+2te^{-t^2}+3t^2e^{2t^3}$. An antiderivative is $e^{5t}-e^{-t^2}+e^{2t^3}/2$. Evaluate at 1 and 0; the endpoint potential $e^x-e^{-y}+e^{2z}/2$ gives the same check." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405742 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406127 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405742 1071 "Source question: 4.\n\nFind the divergence of $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=\\langle7xy^2,7x^2y,7xyz\\rangle$. \n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405742 1071 "Source question: 4.\n\nFind the divergence of $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=\\langle7xy^2,7x^2y,7xyz\\rangle$. \n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405742 1074 "The derivatives are $P_x=7y^2$, $Q_y=7x^2$, $R_z=7xy$. There is no z in the final divergence." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405743 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049639 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405743 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050866 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405744 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050168 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405744 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050537 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405746 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048923 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405746 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050418 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405747 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049889 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405748 1064 "Date imported: 2026-09-30\nDue: 2026-10-02\n\nSource: [WeBWorK — WebWork 1](https://webwork2.science.oregonstate.edu/webwork2/MTH_256_020_F2026/WebWork_1/)\n\nSix problems, each worth 1 point. The equations and initial conditions below are transcribed from the assigned problem versions.\n\nStudy answers, explanations, and reference graphics are authored additions to the linked source." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405748 1064 "Due: 2026-10-02\n\nSource: [WeBWorK — WebWork 1](https://webwork2.science.oregonstate.edu/webwork2/MTH_256_020_F2026/WebWork_1/)" 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405749 1068 #edb/ref 17592186405839 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405749 1068 #edb/ref 17592186405865 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405749 1068 #edb/ref 17592186405867 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405749 1068 #edb/ref 17592186405925 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405749 1068 #edb/ref 17592186405926 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405749 1068 #edb/ref 17592186405936 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405749 1068 #edb/ref 17592186405965 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405749 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406019 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405749 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406151 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405749 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406241 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405749 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406252 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405749 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406285 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405749 1071 "Source question: 1, PDF page 2.\n\nMatch plots A–F to equations (a)–(f) and flow curves (i)–(vi).\n\n![Vector field plots A–F](images/59/59baa4c104233f8ce590e72bb194a56ec065ff68a1220ab92f2e138f39ee43b2.png)\n\n| Equation | Vector field | Curve | Parameterization |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| (a) | $\\langle0,x\\rangle$ | (i) | $\\langle1,t\\rangle$ |\n| (b) | $\\langle y,x\\rangle$ | (ii) | $\\langle\\cos t,\\sin t\\rangle$ |\n| (c) | $\\langle x,y-x\\rangle$ | (iii) | $\\langle\\tan t,\\sec t\\rangle$ |\n| (d) | $\\langle1,2\\rangle$ | (iv) | $\\langle2t,t\\rangle$ |\n| (e) | $\\langle x,y\\rangle$ | (v) | $\\langle t,2t\\rangle$ |\n| (f) | $\\langle-y,x\\rangle$ | (vi) | $\\langle e^t,e^t-te^t\\rangle$ |\n\nEnter the original equation letters and curve Roman numerals.\n\nPlot A: equation ____; curve ____.\n\nPlot B: equation ____; curve ____.\n\nPlot C: equation ____; curve ____.\n\nPlot D: equation ____; curve ____.\n\nPlot E: equation ____; curve ____.\n\nPlot F: equation ____; curve ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405749 1071 "Source question: 1, PDF page 2.\n\nMatch plots A–F to equations (a)–(f) and flow curves (i)–(vi).\n\n![Vector field plots A–F](images/59/59baa4c104233f8ce590e72bb194a56ec065ff68a1220ab92f2e138f39ee43b2.png)\n\n| Equation | Vector field | Curve | Parameterization |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| (a) | $\\langle0,x\\rangle$ | (i) | $\\langle1,t\\rangle$ |\n| (b) | $\\langle y,x\\rangle$ | (ii) | $\\langle\\cos t,\\sin t\\rangle$ |\n| (c) | $\\langle x,y-x\\rangle$ | (iii) | $\\langle\\tan t,\\sec t\\rangle$ |\n| (d) | $\\langle1,2\\rangle$ | (iv) | $\\langle2t,t\\rangle$ |\n| (e) | $\\langle x,y\\rangle$ | (v) | $\\langle t,2t\\rangle$ |\n| (f) | $\\langle-y,x\\rangle$ | (vi) | $\\langle e^t,e^t-te^t\\rangle$ |\n\nEnter the original equation letters and curve Roman numerals.\n\nPlot A: equation {{eq-a}}; curve {{curve-a}}.\n\nPlot B: equation {{eq-b}}; curve {{curve-b}}.\n\nPlot C: equation {{eq-c}}; curve {{curve-c}}.\n\nPlot D: equation {{eq-d}}; curve {{curve-d}}.\n\nPlot E: equation {{eq-e}}; curve {{curve-e}}.\n\nPlot F: equation {{eq-f}}; curve {{curve-f}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405749 1074 "A is radial, B is constant northeast, C rotates counterclockwise, D is vertical with sign set by x, E has components (x,y-x), and F has components (y,x). Thus A–e–iv, B–d–v, C–f–ii, D–a–i, E–c–vi, F–b–iii.\n\nFor (i), (ii), (v), and (vi), direct differentiation gives $\\mathbf r'=\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r)$. For (iii), $\\mathbf r'=\\sec t\\,\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r)$ on its domain. For (iv), $\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r)=t\\mathbf r'$. Thus the last two are geometrically tangent parameterizations, not solutions of $\\mathbf r'=\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r)$ as written. Curve (iv) passes through the equilibrium at t=0 and follows the field orientation only on t>0; treat its two rays separately. On intervals where sec t<0, curve (iii) has reversed orientation. The supplied matching uses geometric flow paths." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405751 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050168 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405751 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050537 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405752 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048225 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405752 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050711 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405753 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406191 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405753 1071 "Source question: 8.\n\nA fluid of constant mass density crosses $z=x^2+y^2$, z≤49, with velocity $\\mathbf F=\\langle-x,-y,-z\\rangle$. Find the mass flow in one unit of time in the direction of the outer normal.\n\n**Source gap:** The constant density has no numerical value. For this study answer, denote it by $\\rho_0$; report signed outward mass flow.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405753 1071 "Source question: 8.\n\nA fluid of constant mass density crosses $z=x^2+y^2$, z≤49, with velocity $\\mathbf F=\\langle-x,-y,-z\\rangle$. Find the mass flow in one unit of time in the direction of the outer normal.\n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405753 1074 "Interpret outward relative to the solid inside the bowl, capped at z=49. On the curved surface the outward vector area is $(2x,2y,-1)dxdy$, so F·dS=−(x²+y²)dxdy. Its integral over r≤7 is $-2\\pi\\int_0^7r^3dr=-2401\\pi/2$. Multiply by the constant density ρ₀. This is signed outward mass flow; the positive mass entering is 2401πρ₀/2. With unit density, omit ρ₀. The source supplies no density value and does not distinguish signed flow from positive mass." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405754 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049465 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405754 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050540 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405756 1074 "**verification:** $\\mathbf r\\prime(t)=\\langle3e^{3t},-1/t^2,1/t\\rangle=\\mathbf F(e^{3t},1/t,\\ln t)$ for t>0.\n\nDifferentiate each coordinate of r and separately substitute r into F. Equality of all three components proves $\\mathbf r\\prime=\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r)$ on the stated domain." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405760 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050338 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405762 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048960 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405762 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049859 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405763 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048651 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405763 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050866 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405765 1068 #edb/ref 17592186405994 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405765 1071 "Source question: 4.\n\nEvaluate $\\oint_C(x^2-13y^3)dx+(x^2+y^2)dy$ counterclockwise around the unit circle $x^2+y^2=1$.\n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405765 1071 "Source question: 4.\n\nEvaluate $\\oint_C(x^2-13y^3)dx+(x^2+y^2)dy$ counterclockwise around the unit circle $x^2+y^2=1$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405765 1074 "$Q_x-P_y=2x+39y^2$. The odd term integrates to zero over the disk; $\\iint y^2dA=\\pi/4$, yielding 39π/4." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405770 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406093 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405770 1071 "Source question: 6.\n\nFind $\\partial(x,y,z)/\\partial(u,v,w)$ for $x=3(u+v)$, $y=-5u+7w$, $z=6u^5-11w^{10}$.\n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405770 1071 "Source question: 6.\n\nFind $\\partial(x,y,z)/\\partial(u,v,w)$ for $x=3(u+v)$, $y=-5u+7w$, $z=6u^5-11w^{10}$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405770 1074 "The matrix is $\\begin{pmatrix}3&3&0\\\\-5&0&7\\\\30u^4&0&-110w^9\\end{pmatrix}$. Expand along the second column: $-3(550w^9-210u^4)=630u^4-1650w^9$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405771 1068 #edb/ref 17592186405837 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405771 1071 "**(a)** C consists of the line segment(s) joining $(7\\pi,0)\\to(8\\pi,1)$, in that order.\n\nIntegral: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405771 1071 "**(a)** C consists of the line segment(s) joining $(7\\pi,0)\\to(8\\pi,1)$, in that order.\n\nIntegral: {{integral}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405771 1074 "The potential is $f=e^{2y}\\sin x$. Both endpoint values vanish, because sin(7π)=sin(8π)=0. Each path therefore has integral 0; intermediate contributions cancel." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405777 1074 "Let $P(x)$ be any antiderivative of $p(x)$. Then $\\frac{d}{dx}e^{P(x)}=P\\prime(x)e^{P(x)}=p(x)e^{P(x)}=p(x)\\exp(\\int p(x)\\,dx)$.\n\nApply the exponential chain rule and then the definition $P\\prime=p$. The additive constant in $P$ only changes a constant multiplicative factor in the exponential; the displayed derivative identity remains valid." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405778 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048225 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405778 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050488 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405779 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406041 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405779 1071 "Source question: 15.\n\nFind $\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot d\\mathbf r$ for $\\mathbf F=\\langle16x^2,xy\\rangle$, $\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle\\sin t,-4\\cos t\\rangle$, $0\\le t\\le\\pi$.\n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405779 1071 "Source question: 15.\n\nFind $\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot d\\mathbf r$ for $\\mathbf F=\\langle16x^2,xy\\rangle$, $\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle\\sin t,-4\\cos t\\rangle$, $0\\le t\\le\\pi$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405779 1074 "$F(r)=(16\\sin^2t,-4\\sin t\\cos t)$ and $r\\prime=(\\cos t,4\\sin t)$. Their dot product is identically zero. This checks the integral without evaluating an antiderivative." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405780 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048575 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405780 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049152 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405781 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049465 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405781 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050540 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405782 1074 "**drawing:** Draw the radius-a circle and equal-length arrows circulating counterclockwise around the origin. At (a,0), (0,a), (−a,0), (0,−a), arrows point up, left, down, right. The field is undefined at the origin.\n\n![Authored tangential field reference](images/5c/5c6173bdda7a93fc42142645d1013b63a881e47510dcde4ea6ae142354a2b7d9.png)\n\n$(-y,x)$ is (x,y) rotated 90 degrees counterclockwise. Its magnitude is $\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}$, so normalization gives unit arrows." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405783 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049487 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405783 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050537 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405783 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050866 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405785 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048575 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405785 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049487 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405785 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050614 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405786 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049465 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405786 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050540 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405787 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406050 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405787 1071 "Source question: 12.\n\nUse a change of variables to find the area enclosed by $x+y=4$, $x+y=-7$, $y=7x$, and $y=7x+7$. Give the area in square units.\n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405787 1071 "Source question: 12.\n\nUse a change of variables to find the area enclosed by $x+y=4$, $x+y=-7$, $y=7x$, and $y=7x+7$. Give the area in square units.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405787 1074 "Choose u=x+y and v=y−7x. The new rectangle has −7≤u≤4 and 0≤v≤7. Inverting gives x=(u−v)/8,y=(7u+v)/8 and $|J|=1/8$. The area is 11·7/8=77/8 square units." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405791 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406078 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405791 1071 "**Question 2 — Part C**\n\n$y'(x)=5\\sin(x),\\quad y(0)=10$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405791 1071 "**Question 2 — Part C**\n\n$y'(x)=5\\sin(x),\\quad y(0)=10$.\n\n$y(x)=$ {{field-1}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405791 1074 "An antiderivative of $5\\sin x$ is $-5\\cos x$. Setting $-5+C=10$ gives $C=15$, and $y\\prime=5\\sin x$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405796 1064 "**5.7 Change of Variables in Multiple Integrals**\n\nDue: 2026-11-12\nSource: [Macmillan Achieve](https://achieve.macmillanlearning.com/ihub/assessment/f188d950-dd73-11e0-9572-0800200c9a66/5a1db2a7-4295-4a4c-9dbb-ceb8b174a282/d5d2efff-1127-4196-836e-e717fd7546a3)" 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405796 1064 "**5.7 Change of Variables in Multiple Integrals**\n\nDue: 2026-11-12\nSource: [Macmillan Achieve](https://achieve.macmillanlearning.com/ihub/assessment/f188d950-dd73-11e0-9572-0800200c9a66/5a1db2a7-4295-4a4c-9dbb-ceb8b174a282/d5d2efff-1127-4196-836e-e717fd7546a3)\n\nStudy answers and explanations are authored additions, not publisher solutions.\n\n## Instructions\n\nGive exact answers using symbolic notation and fractions. Use the stated substitutions in Questions 7–9 and 11; use a change of variables in Question 12. Source: Sullivan 2e Calculus, W.H. Freeman. Unlimited attempts, no incorrect-attempt penalty; up to 50 days late with a 2% daily penalty." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405798 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406196 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405798 1071 "**Question 1 — Part A**\n\nFind the rate of change of the voltage at $P$ in the direction of $\\mathbf v$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405798 1071 "**Question 1 — Part A**\n\nFind the rate of change of the voltage at $P$ in the direction of $\\mathbf v$.\n\nRate: {{field-1}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405798 1074 "$\\nabla V=\\langle3x^2-5y+yz,-5x+xz,xy\\rangle$, so $\\nabla V(P)=\\langle12,0,16\\rangle$. Normalize the given direction: $\\mathbf u=\\langle1,1,-1\\rangle/\\sqrt3$. Then $D_{\\mathbf u}V=\\nabla V(P)\\cdot\\mathbf u=(12-16)/\\sqrt3=-4/\\sqrt3$. The negative sign means voltage decreases in that direction." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405802 1068 #edb/ref 17592186405961 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405802 1071 "Source question: 8.\n\nThe field $\\mathbf F=\\langle\\ln y+12x,x/y\\rangle$ is conservative. Find a potential f. Use K for the arbitrary constant and absorb into K as much as possible.\n\n$f(x,y)=$ ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405802 1071 "Source question: 8.\n\nThe field $\\mathbf F=\\langle\\ln y+12x,x/y\\rangle$ is conservative. Find a potential f. Use K for the arbitrary constant and absorb into K as much as possible.\n\n$f(x,y)=$ {{potential}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405802 1074 "For y>0, integration in x gives $f=x\\ln y+6x^2+g(y)$. Then $f_y=x/y+g′(y)$, so g′=0. The domain condition y>0 comes from ln y." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405803 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050168 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405803 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050537 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405804 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049465 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405805 1068 #edb/ref 17592186405941 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405805 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406010 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405805 1071 "**(d)** $\\phi=\\frac{\\ln(x^2+y^2+z^2)}2$. Find its gradient field and the points excluded from its domain.\n\nGradient: ____.\n\nExcluded points: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405805 1071 "**(d)** $\\phi=\\frac{\\ln(x^2+y^2+z^2)}2$. Find its gradient field and the points excluded from its domain.\n\nGradient: {{gradient}}.\n\nExcluded points: {{excluded}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405805 1074 "The logarithm requires $x^2+y^2+z^2>0$. Differentiating gives $\\phi_x=x/(x^2+y^2+z^2)$ and analogous y,z components. No additional points are lost." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405808 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048960 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405808 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049138 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405808 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050033 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405808 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050037 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405808 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050164 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405809 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048923 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405809 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050418 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405810 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406155 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405810 1071 "Source question: 1.\n\nFind the divergence of $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=\\langle9x^2,9y^2,3z^4\\rangle$. \n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405810 1071 "Source question: 1.\n\nFind the divergence of $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=\\langle9x^2,9y^2,3z^4\\rangle$. \n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405810 1074 "The three same-coordinate derivatives are 18x, 18y, and 12z³. Add them; divergence is a scalar." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405812 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406169 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405812 1071 "Source question: 4.\n\nFind $\\partial(x,y,z)/\\partial(u,v,w)$ for $x=2u+8v+4w$, $y=13u+4v+6w$, $z=14u+6v+12w$.\n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405812 1071 "Source question: 4.\n\nFind $\\partial(x,y,z)/\\partial(u,v,w)$ for $x=2u+8v+4w$, $y=13u+4v+6w$, $z=14u+6v+12w$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405812 1074 "The determinant of $\\begin{pmatrix}2&8&4\\\\13&4&6\\\\14&6&12\\end{pmatrix}$ expands to $2(48-36)-8(156-84)+4(78-56)=24-576+88=-464$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405813 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048314 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405813 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050149 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405814 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049465 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405814 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050540 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405815 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406110 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405815 1071 "**Question 4 — Part B**\n\nChoose the graph with the correct curve and direction." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405815 1071 "**Question 4 — Part B**\n\nChoose the graph with the correct curve and direction.\n\n1. ![Choice 1](images/d8/d8c88faa66f299ab520136887210567700ad34f4a25e0fbfd80a8e513a92ebae.png)\n\n2. ![Choice 2](images/91/913130e84d7777315aef2a2d0937e96d897fed6d3ff17e6fcc447a321cc89da7.png)\n\n3. ![Choice 3](images/bf/bf1ef84e20ef40a580e31e821168d82b88224aef7765a8310d253fa2380adb22.png)\n\n4. ![Choice 4](images/6a/6aa3ec9f041275f7830bbecdf8a19ba1481442400b8b718dc22629ed261a73b6.png)" 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405815 1074 "The circle has center $(0,0)$ and radius $5$. At $t=0$, the point is $(0,5)$ and the velocity is $(5,0)$, so it moves clockwise." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405816 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050149 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405816 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050891 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405818 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050891 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405820 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048923 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405820 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050418 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405822 1074 "In the upper-left part of the supplied field, trace the upper arc of a small closed trajectory surrounding approximately $(-2,0)$. Use only the portion above the $x$-axis between its left and right vertical turns, excluding those endpoints. Label this arc “solution curve.” It follows the local directions and gives one $y$ for each $x$ on that open interval.\n\nUse the displayed field to trace the arc; no exact equation is supplied. A solution graph must be single-valued and have finite derivative on its interval, so stop before the vertical turns rather than including a complete loop." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405823 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048960 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405823 1089 #edb/ref 17592186049859 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405824 1068 #edb/ref 17592186405885 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405824 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406122 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405824 1071 "Source question: 10.\n\nDetermine whether $\\mathbf F=\\langle x^2+y^{10},10xy^9-\\sin y\\rangle$ is conservative. Then find a potential f, using C for the arbitrary constant; enter DNE if it is not conservative.\n\n$f(x,y)=$ ____.\n\n1. The field is conservative.\n\n2. The field is not conservative." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405824 1071 "Source question: 10.\n\nDetermine whether $\\mathbf F=\\langle x^2+y^{10},10xy^9-\\sin y\\rangle$ is conservative. Then find a potential f, using C for the arbitrary constant; enter DNE if it is not conservative.\n\n$f(x,y)=$ {{potential}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405824 1074 "Integrate P in x: $f=x^3/3+xy^{10}+g(y)$. Matching Q yields g′=−sin y, hence g=cos y+C. Its gradient checks both components." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405826 1064 "Date: 2026-09-29\nDue: 2026-09-29\n\nSource: [Original worksheet](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F255%2FR1%2F255-R1.pdf)" 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405826 1064 "Date: 2026-09-29\nDue: 2026-09-29\n\nSource: [Original worksheet](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F255%2FR1%2F255-R1.pdf)\n\n## Instructions\n\nRecitation worksheets are graded for completeness. Complete the worksheet on the printed pages, digitally on the PDF, or on your own paper with the same problem layout. Submit a single PDF to Gradescope under **Recitation Worksheet 1**, with the cover page followed by one page per problem, including blank problems. If a solution spans multiple pages, merge those pages into a single page for that problem. See the original PDF for the full submission instructions.\n\nStudy answers, explanations, and reference graphics are authored additions to the linked source." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405827 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406225 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405827 1071 "Source question: 2.\n\nEvaluate $\\oint_C2x\\,dy$ counterclockwise around the rectangle with vertices (0,0), (6,0), (6,2), (0,2).\n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405827 1071 "Source question: 2.\n\nEvaluate $\\oint_C2x\\,dy$ counterclockwise around the rectangle with vertices (0,0), (6,0), (6,2), (0,2).\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405827 1074 "$Q_x-P_y=2$. The enclosed area is 6·2=12, giving 24. The right side contributes $\\int_0^2 12dy=24$, and the left side contributes zero." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405828 1068 #edb/ref 17592186405897 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405828 1071 "Source question: 11.\n\nFind $\\oint_C y^3dx-x^3dy$, where C is the boundary of the region between $x^2+y^2=4$ and $x^2+y^2=1$.\n\nAnswer: ____." 13194139533503 17592186405015 false] [17592186405828 1071 "Source question: 11.\n\nFind $\\oint_C y^3dx-x^3dy$, where C is the boundary of the region between $x^2+y^2=4$ and $x^2+y^2=1$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405828 1074 "Using the standard positive boundary orientation (outer circle counterclockwise, inner circle clockwise), $Q_x-P_y=-3r^2$. Thus $I=-3\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\int_1^2r^3drd\\theta=-45\\pi/2$. The source does not explicitly specify orientation; reversing the full boundary changes the sign." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405830 1089 #edb/ref 17592186048259 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405832 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050537 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405832 1089 #edb/ref 17592186050866 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405833 1068 #edb/ref 17592186405846 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405833 1071 "Source question: 3.\n\n**Question 3**\n\nChoose the graph of $\\mathbf F(x,y)=x\\mathbf i+y\\mathbf j$." 13194139533503 17592186405015 true] [17592186405833 1071 "Source question: 3.\n\n**Question 3**\n\nChoose the graph of $\\mathbf F(x,y)=x\\mathbf i+y\\mathbf j$.\n\n1. ![Choice 1](images/ce/ceba1e47532b41d313ce9ae2de01278ff3603154ffe070cd680a3859eab7de58.png)\n\n2. ![Choice 2](images/0c/0c67b07111ce3e2d9d19586df1c6bf2b73c5c30c64c647013d9c1ebcbd5bc390.png)\n\